/* NCERT Physics Class XI — Chapter 2: Motion in a Straight Line (book page 13–26)
   type 'num' = Numerical (step-by-step solution in sol); img = inline SVG graph/diagram */
addChapter({
  id: 'phy-11-02',
  subj: 'phy',
  cls: 11,
  no: 2,
  title: 'Motion in a Straight Line',
  src: 'NCERT Physics XI · Chapter 2',
  points: [
    { id: 'in1', topic: '2.1 Introduction', t: 'Motion is common to everything in the universe: we walk, run and ride a bicycle; even when we are sleeping, air moves into and out of our lungs and blood flows in arteries and veins. The earth rotates once every twenty-four hours and revolves round the sun once in a year. The Sun itself is in motion in the Milky Way, which is again moving within its local group of galaxies.' },
    { id: 'in2', topic: 'Rectilinear motion', t: 'Motion is change in position of an object with time. In this chapter we study motion of objects along a straight line, also known as rectilinear motion; for rectilinear motion with uniform acceleration a set of simple equations can be obtained. To understand the relative nature of motion, the concept of relative velocity is introduced.' },
    { id: 'in3', topic: 'Point object', t: 'Objects in motion are treated as point objects. This approximation is valid so far as the size of the object is much smaller than the distance it moves in a reasonable duration of time. In many real-life situations the size of objects can be neglected without much error.' },
    { id: 'in4', topic: 'Kinematics', t: 'In Kinematics, we study ways to describe motion without going into the causes of motion. What causes motion forms the subject matter of Chapter 4.' },

    { id: 'iv1', topic: '2.2 Instantaneous velocity', t: 'Average velocity tells how fast an object has been moving over a time interval but not how fast it moves at different instants. Instantaneous velocity v is the limit of the average velocity as the time interval Δt becomes infinitesimally small: v = lim (Δt→0) Δx/Δt = dx/dt, the differential coefficient of x with respect to t — the rate of change of position with respect to time at that instant.' },
    { id: 'iv2', topic: 'Graphical method', t: 'Velocity at an instant can be obtained graphically or numerically. For the car of Fig 2.1, taking Δt = 2 s centred at t = 4 s, slope of line P₁P₂ gives the average velocity over 3 s to 5 s; with Δt = 1 s, Q₁Q₂ gives it over 3.5 s to 4.5 s. In the limit Δt → 0, P₁P₂ becomes tangent to the x–t curve at P, and velocity at t = 4 s is the slope of the tangent at that point.' },
    { id: 'f21', topic: 'Fig 2.1', t: 'Fig 2.1 Determining velocity from position–time graph for x = 0.08 t³: velocity at t = 4 s is the slope of the tangent to the graph at that instant (points P₁, Q₁, T₁, P, T₂, Q₂, P₂ shown on the curve).' },
    { id: 't21', topic: 'Table 2.1', t: 'Table 2.1 Limiting value of Δx/Δt at t = 4 s, with t₁ = t − Δt/2, t₂ = t + Δt/2: Δt = 2.0 s → 3.92; 1.0 s → 3.86; 0.5 s → 3.845; 0.1 s → 3.8402; 0.01 s → 3.8400 m s⁻¹. As Δt decreases, average velocity approaches the limiting value 3.84 m s⁻¹, the value of dx/dt at t = 4.0 s.' },
    { id: 'iv3', topic: 'Numerical method', t: 'The graphical method is not always convenient. It is easier to calculate velocity at different instants if we have data of positions at different instants or the exact expression for position as a function of time — then we calculate Δx/Δt for decreasing Δt or use differential calculus to find dx/dt.' },
    { id: 'ex21', topic: 'Example 2.1', t: 'Example 2.1: x = a + bt² with a = 8.5 m, b = 2.5 m s⁻². v = dx/dt = 2bt = 5.0 t m s⁻¹; at t = 0, v = 0 and at t = 2.0 s, v = 10 m s⁻¹. Average velocity between 2.0 s and 4.0 s = [x(4.0) − x(2.0)]/(4.0 − 2.0) = 6.0 × b = 15 m s⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'iv4', topic: 'Uniform motion', t: 'For uniform motion, velocity is the same as the average velocity at all instants.' },
    { id: 'sp1', topic: 'Speed', t: 'Instantaneous speed or simply speed is the magnitude of velocity: velocities +24.0 m s⁻¹ and −24.0 m s⁻¹ both have speed 24.0 m s⁻¹. Average speed over a finite interval is greater than or equal to the magnitude of average velocity, but instantaneous speed at an instant equals the magnitude of instantaneous velocity at that instant.' },

    { id: 'ac1', topic: '2.3 Acceleration — Galileo', t: 'It was first thought that change in velocity could be described by the rate of change of velocity with distance. Through studies of freely falling objects and motion on an inclined plane, Galileo concluded that the rate of change of velocity with time is a constant for all objects in free fall, while change in velocity with distance is not constant — it decreases with increasing distance of fall. This led to acceleration as the rate of change of velocity with time.' },
    { id: 'ac2', topic: 'Average acceleration', t: 'Average acceleration ā = (v₂ − v₁)/(t₂ − t₁) = Δv/Δt — the average change of velocity per unit time. SI unit is m s⁻². On a plot of velocity versus time, average acceleration is the slope of the straight line connecting (v₂, t₂) and (v₁, t₁).' },
    { id: 'ac3', topic: 'Instantaneous acceleration', t: 'Instantaneous acceleration a = lim (Δt→0) Δv/Δt = dv/dt. The acceleration at an instant is the slope of the tangent to the v–t curve at that instant.' },
    { id: 'ac4', topic: 'Sign of acceleration', t: 'Since velocity has both magnitude and direction, a change in velocity may involve either or both; acceleration may result from a change in speed (magnitude), a change in direction or changes in both. Like velocity, acceleration can be positive, negative or zero.' },
    { id: 'f22', topic: 'Fig 2.2', t: 'Fig 2.2 Position–time graphs: the graph curves upward for positive acceleration, downward for negative acceleration and is a straight line for zero acceleration.' },
    { id: 'ac5', topic: 'Constant acceleration', t: 'Study in this chapter is restricted to motion with constant acceleration; then the average acceleration equals the constant value of acceleration during the interval. If velocity is v₀ at t = 0 and v at time t: ā = (v − v₀)/(t − 0), or v = v₀ + at (Eq. 2.4).' },
    { id: 'f23', topic: 'Fig 2.3', t: 'Fig 2.3 Velocity–time graphs for constant acceleration: (a) motion in positive direction with positive acceleration; (b) positive direction with negative acceleration; (c) negative direction with negative acceleration; (d) negative acceleration that changes direction at t₁ — between 0 and t₁ it moves in positive x-direction and between t₁ and t₂ in the opposite direction.' },
    { id: 'ac6', topic: 'Area under v–t curve', t: 'The area under the velocity–time curve represents the displacement over a given time interval. For constant velocity u (Fig 2.4) the v–t curve is a straight line parallel to the time axis and the area between t = 0 and t = T is the rectangle u × T = uT, which is the displacement.' },
    { id: 'kk1', topic: 'Kinks in graphs', t: 'x–t, v–t and a–t graphs with sharp kinks at some points imply that the functions are not differentiable there. In any realistic situation the functions are differentiable at all points and the graphs are smooth: acceleration and velocity cannot change values abruptly at an instant; changes are always continuous.' },

    { id: 'ke1', topic: '2.4 Kinematic equations — Fig 2.5', t: 'For uniformly accelerated motion, simple equations relate displacement x, time taken t, initial velocity v₀, final velocity v and acceleration a. From Fig 2.5, area under v–t curve between 0 and t = area of triangle ABC + area of rectangle OACD = ½(v − v₀)t + v₀t, which is the displacement x.' },
    { id: 'ke2', topic: 'x = v₀t + ½at²', t: 'Since v − v₀ = at, x = ½at² + v₀t, or x = v₀t + ½at² (Eq. 2.6).' },
    { id: 'ke3', topic: 'Average velocity', t: 'Eq. 2.5 can be written as x = [(v + v₀)/2] t = v̄t, where v̄ = (v + v₀)/2 (constant acceleration only). The object has undergone displacement x with an average velocity equal to the arithmetic average of the initial and final velocities.' },
    { id: 'ke4', topic: 'v² = v₀² + 2ax', t: 'Substituting t = (v − v₀)/a from Eq. 2.4 into Eq. 2.7a: x = [(v + v₀)/2][(v − v₀)/a] = (v² − v₀²)/2a, i.e. v² = v₀² + 2ax (Eq. 2.8). It can also be obtained by substituting t from Eq. 2.4 into Eq. 2.6.' },
    { id: 'ke5', topic: 'Equations 2.9', t: 'v = v₀ + at, x = v₀t + ½at², v² = v₀² + 2ax connect five quantities v₀, v, a, t and x — the kinematic equations of rectilinear motion for constant acceleration. They assume that at t = 0 the position x is 0. If the position at t = 0 is x₀, x is replaced by (x − x₀): x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at², v² = v₀² + 2a(x − x₀).' },
    { id: 'ex22', topic: 'Example 2.2 Calculus', t: 'Example 2.2: integrating a = dv/dt gives v − v₀ = at; integrating v = dx/dt gives x − x₀ = v₀t + ½at². Writing a = dv/dt = (dv/dx)(dx/dt) = v dv/dx, v dv = a dx; integrating, (v² − v₀²)/2 = a(x − x₀). The advantage of this method is that it can be used for motion with non-uniform acceleration also.' },
    { id: 'ex23a', topic: 'Example 2.3 — height', t: 'Example 2.3: a ball is thrown vertically upwards with 20 m s⁻¹ from the top of a building 25.0 m high (g = 10 m s⁻²). Taking y-axis vertically upward with zero at the ground, v₀ = +20 m s⁻¹, a = −10 m s⁻², v = 0 at top; from v² = v₀² + 2a(y − y₀): 0 = (20)² + 2(−10)(y − y₀), so the ball rises (y − y₀) = 20 m.' },
    { id: 'ex23b', topic: 'Example 2.3 — time', t: 'First method: upward motion A to B takes t₁ = 2 s (0 = 20 − 10t₁); from B (y₀ = 45 m) the ball falls freely, 0 = 45 + ½(−10)t₂², t₂ = 3 s; total 5 s. Second method: y = y₀ + v₀t + ½at² with y₀ = 25 m, y = 0: 0 = 25 + 20t − 5t², i.e. 5t² − 20t − 25 = 0, t = 5 s. The second method is better since we do not have to worry about the path, as the motion is under constant acceleration.' },
    { id: 'f26', topic: 'Fig 2.6', t: 'Fig 2.6: building of height 25 m; ball launched at A (top of building), rises (y − y₀) to the highest point B and falls to C on the ground; origin 0 at the ground, y measured upward, a = −10 m s⁻².' },
    { id: 'ff1', topic: 'Example 2.4 Free fall', t: 'An object released near the surface of the Earth is accelerated downward under the force of gravity; magnitude of acceleration due to gravity is g. If air resistance is neglected, the object is in free fall. If the height of fall is small compared to the earth\'s radius, g can be taken as constant, 9.8 m s⁻². Free fall is thus a case of motion with uniform acceleration.' },
    { id: 'ff2', topic: 'Free fall equations', t: 'Choosing upward direction as positive, a = −g = −9.8 m s⁻². For an object released from rest at y = 0 (v₀ = 0): v = 0 − gt = −9.8t m s⁻¹; y = 0 − ½gt² = −4.9t² m; v² = 0 − 2gy = −19.6y m² s⁻².' },
    { id: 'f27', topic: 'Fig 2.7', t: 'Fig 2.7 Motion of an object under free fall: (a) acceleration–time — a horizontal line at −9.8 m s⁻²; (b) velocity–time — a straight line from 0 going down to about −49 m s⁻¹ at 5 s; (c) distance (y)–time — a parabola curving downward (about −78 m at 4 s).' },
    { id: 'gl1', topic: 'Example 2.5 Odd numbers', t: 'Galileo\'s law of odd numbers: the distances traversed, during equal intervals of time, by a body falling from rest stand to one another in the same ratio as the odd numbers beginning with unity, 1 : 3 : 5 : 7 … This law was established by Galileo Galilei (1564–1642), who was the first to make quantitative studies of free fall.' },
    { id: 't22', topic: 'Table 2.2', t: 'Table 2.2: with y = −½gt² and y₀ = (−½)gτ², positions after τ, 2τ, 3τ, 4τ, 5τ, 6τ are y₀, 4y₀, 9y₀, 16y₀, 25y₀, 36y₀; distances traversed in successive intervals are y₀, 3y₀, 5y₀, 7y₀, 9y₀, 11y₀ — ratio 1 : 3 : 5 : 7 : 9 : 11.' },
    { id: 'sd1', topic: 'Example 2.6 Stopping distance', t: 'When brakes are applied to a moving vehicle, the distance it travels before stopping is the stopping distance — an important factor for road safety. It depends on the initial velocity v₀ and the braking capacity (deceleration −a). Using v² = v₀² + 2ax with v = 0, dₛ = −v₀²/2a. Stopping distance is proportional to the square of the initial velocity: doubling v₀ increases it by a factor of 4 (same deceleration).' },
    { id: 'sd2', topic: 'Braking data', t: 'For the car of a particular make, braking distances were 10 m, 20 m, 34 m and 50 m for velocities of 11, 15, 20 and 25 m s⁻¹ — nearly consistent with the formula. Stopping distance is an important factor considered in setting speed limits, for example, in school zones.' },
    { id: 'rt1', topic: 'Example 2.7 Reaction time', t: 'Reaction time is the time a person takes to observe, think and act — e.g., the time elapsed before a driver slams the brakes when a boy suddenly appears on the road. Reaction time depends on complexity of the situation and on an individual.' },
    { id: 'rt2', topic: 'Measuring reaction time (Fig 2.8)', t: 'Fig 2.8: a friend drops a ruler vertically through the gap between your thumb and forefinger and you catch it; the distance d travelled by the ruler is measured. The ruler is in free fall (v₀ = 0, a = −g), so d = ½g tᵣ² (magnitude) and tᵣ = √(2d/g). For d = 21.0 cm and g = 9.8 m s⁻², tᵣ = √(2 × 0.21/9.8) s ≅ 0.2 s.' },

    { id: 'su1', topic: 'Summary', t: 'An object is in motion if its position changes with time. Position is specified with reference to a conveniently chosen origin; for motion in a straight line, position to the right of the origin is taken as positive and to the left as negative. The average speed of an object is greater than or equal to the magnitude of the average velocity over a given time interval.' },
    { id: 'su2', topic: 'Summary — graphs', t: 'For uniform motion, acceleration is zero, the x–t graph is a straight line inclined to the time axis and the v–t graph is a straight line parallel to the time axis. For motion with uniform acceleration, the x–t graph is a parabola while the v–t graph is a straight line inclined to the time axis. The area under the v–t curve between t₁ and t₂ equals the displacement in that interval.' },
    { id: 'tq1', topic: 'Table — path length, displacement', t: 'Table of quantities: path length — dimension [L], unit m; displacement Δx = x₂ − x₁ — dimension [L], unit m; in one dimension its sign indicates the direction.' },
    { id: 'tq2', topic: 'Table — velocity, speed, acceleration', t: 'Velocity [LT⁻¹], m s⁻¹: average v̄ = Δx/Δt, instantaneous v = dx/dt. Speed [LT⁻¹], m s⁻¹: average = path length/time interval, instantaneous = dx/dt (magnitude). Acceleration [LT⁻²], m s⁻²: average ā = Δv/Δt, instantaneous a = dv/dt. In one dimension the sign of velocity and acceleration indicates the direction.' },

    { id: 'pp1', topic: 'Points to ponder 1', t: 'The origin and the positive direction of an axis are a matter of choice. You should first specify this choice before you assign signs to quantities like displacement, velocity and acceleration.' },
    { id: 'pp2', topic: 'Points to ponder 2', t: 'If a particle is speeding up, acceleration is in the direction of velocity; if its speed is decreasing, acceleration is in the direction opposite to that of the velocity. This statement is independent of the choice of the origin and the axis.' },
    { id: 'pp3', topic: 'Points to ponder 3', t: 'The sign of acceleration does not tell whether the particle\'s speed is increasing or decreasing; it depends on the choice of positive direction of the axis. If upward is positive, g is negative: for a particle falling under gravity this negative acceleration results in increase in speed, while for a particle thrown upward the same negative acceleration results in decrease in speed.' },
    { id: 'pp4', topic: 'Points to ponder 4', t: 'Zero velocity of a particle at any instant does not necessarily imply zero acceleration at that instant. A particle thrown up has zero velocity at its uppermost point but the acceleration at that instant continues to be the acceleration due to gravity.' },
    { id: 'pp5', topic: 'Points to ponder 5', t: 'In the kinematic equations of motion (Eq. 2.9), the various quantities are algebraic, i.e. they may be positive or negative. The equations are applicable in all situations (one-dimensional motion with constant acceleration) provided values are substituted with proper signs.' },
    { id: 'pp6', topic: 'Points to ponder 6', t: 'The definitions of instantaneous velocity and acceleration (Eqs. 2.1 and 2.3) are exact and always correct, while the kinematic equations (Eq. 2.9) are true only for motion in which the magnitude and the direction of acceleration are constant during the course of motion.' },

    { id: 'xe1', topic: 'Exercise 2.1', t: 'Exercise 2.1: a railway carriage moving without jerks between two stations and a monkey sitting on top of a man cycling smoothly on a circular track can be considered point objects; a spinning cricket ball that turns sharply on hitting the ground and a tumbling beaker slipping off a table cannot.' },
    { id: 'xe2', topic: 'Exercise 2.2 (Fig 2.9)', t: 'Exercise 2.2 / Fig 2.9: x–t graphs of children A and B returning from school O to homes P and Q. A lives closer to the school (P < Q); A starts earlier (B starts later); B walks faster (steeper line); B overtakes A once on the road; they reach home at different times.' },
    { id: 'xe3', topic: 'Exercise 2.5', t: 'Exercise 2.5: a car moving at 126 km h⁻¹ (35 m s⁻¹) is brought to a stop within 200 m. Retardation a = v₀²/2s = 35²/400 ≈ 3.06 m s⁻²; time to stop t = v₀/a ≈ 11.4 s.' },
    { id: 'xe4', topic: 'Exercise 2.6', t: 'Exercise 2.6: a ball thrown upward with 29.4 m s⁻¹ (g = 9.8 m s⁻²): acceleration is vertically downward throughout; at the highest point velocity is zero and acceleration is 9.8 m s⁻² downward; it rises 44.1 m and returns to the player\'s hands after 6 s.' },
    { id: 'xe5', topic: 'Exercise 2.7', t: 'Exercise 2.7: a particle in one-dimensional motion with zero speed at an instant may have non-zero acceleration (true); with zero speed cannot have non-zero velocity; with constant speed must have zero acceleration (true in one dimension); with positive acceleration need not be speeding up (false — e.g. negative velocity with positive acceleration slows down).' },
    { id: 'xe6', topic: 'Exercise 2.10', t: 'Exercise 2.10: a man walks 2.5 km to a market at 5 km h⁻¹, finds it closed and walks back at 7.5 km h⁻¹. 0–30 min: |average velocity| = average speed = 5 km h⁻¹; 0–50 min: average velocity 0, average speed 6 km h⁻¹; 0–40 min: |average velocity| 1.875 km h⁻¹, average speed 5.625 km h⁻¹. Hence average speed is defined as total path length divided by time.' },
    { id: 'xe7', topic: 'Exercise 2.12 (Fig 2.10)', t: 'Exercise 2.12 / Fig 2.10: none of the graphs can represent one-dimensional motion — (a) shows two positions at the same instant, (b) two velocities at the same instant, (c) negative speed, (d) total path length decreasing with time.' },
    { id: 'xe8', topic: 'Exercise 2.14', t: 'Exercise 2.14: a police van at 30 km h⁻¹ fires a bullet (muzzle speed 150 m s⁻¹) at a thief\'s car speeding away at 192 km h⁻¹ in the same direction. Speed relevant for damage = speed of bullet relative to the car = 150 + 8.33 − 53.33 = 105 m s⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'xe9', topic: 'Exercise 2.4', t: 'Exercise 2.4: a drunkard takes 5 steps forward and 3 steps backward repeatedly, each step 1 m long taking 1 s. Every 8 s he moves 2 m net; after 32 s he is at 8 m and 5 more forward steps take him to the pit 13 m away at t = 37 s.' },
    { id: 'xe10', topic: 'Exercise 2.3', t: 'Exercise 2.3: a woman leaves home at 9.00 am, walks at 5 km h⁻¹ to her office 2.5 km away (reaches 9.30 am), stays up to 5.00 pm (x–t graph parallel to time axis) and returns by auto at 25 km h⁻¹, taking 6 min (reaches home 5.06 pm).' },
    { id: 'xe11', topic: 'Exercise 2.13 (Fig 2.11)', t: 'Exercise 2.13 / Fig 2.11: x–t plot with x = 0 for t < 0 and a parabola for t > 0. It is not correct to say the particle moves on a parabolic path; the x–t graph does not show the path. The particle is at rest for t < 0 and moves in a straight line with uniform acceleration for t > 0 (e.g. a car starting from rest).' }
  ],
  qs: [
    { id: 'q1', p: ['in1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The earth rotates once in about ___ and revolves round the sun once in ___.', o: ['24 hours; a month', '24 hours; a year', '12 hours; a year', 'a year; 24 hours'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT 2.1.' },
    { id: 'q2', p: ['in1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Even when we are sleeping, air moves into and out of our lungs and blood flows in arteries and veins.', 'The Sun is at rest in the Milky Way.'], a: 2, e: 'The Sun itself is in motion in the Milky Way.' },
    { id: 'q3', p: ['in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Motion of an object along a straight line is also known as:', o: ['Circular motion', 'Projectile motion', 'Rotational motion', 'Rectilinear motion'], a: 3, e: 'Rectilinear motion.' },
    { id: 'q4', p: ['in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Motion is defined as:', o: ['Change in position of an object with time', 'Change in mass of an object with time', 'Change in shape of an object', 'Force acting per unit time'], a: 0, e: 'Motion is change in position with time.' },
    { id: 'q5', p: ['in3', 'xe1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A railway carriage moving without jerks between two stations can be treated as a point object.', R: 'The point-object approximation is valid when the size of the object is much smaller than the distance it moves in a reasonable duration of time.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q6', p: ['in3', 'xe1'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'In which of the following can the body be considered approximately a point object?', s: ['A railway carriage moving without jerks between two stations.', 'A monkey sitting on top of a man cycling smoothly on a circular track.', 'A spinning cricket ball that turns sharply on hitting the ground.', 'A tumbling beaker that has slipped off the edge of a table.'], o: ['I, II and III only', 'III and IV only', 'I and II only', 'II and IV only'], a: 2, e: 'In III and IV the size and spinning/tumbling of the body matter.' },
    { id: 'q7', p: ['in4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Kinematics is the study of:', o: ['Causes of motion', 'Forces on bodies at rest', 'Ways to describe motion without going into its causes', 'Energy of moving bodies'], a: 2, e: 'Causes of motion come in Chapter 4.' },
    { id: 'q8', p: ['in4'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['In kinematics we study the causes of motion.', 'What causes motion is the subject matter of kinematics.'], a: 1, e: 'Kinematics only describes motion; causes come in Chapter 4.' },

    { id: 'q9', p: ['iv1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Instantaneous velocity is defined as:', o: ['Total displacement / total time', 'Δv/Δt', 'Path length / time interval', 'lim (Δt→0) Δx/Δt = dx/dt'], a: 3, e: 'Limit of average velocity as Δt → 0.' },
    { id: 'q10', p: ['iv1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'The position of a particle moving along x-axis is x = 3t² + 2t + 5 (x in m, t in s). Its velocity at t = 2 s is:', o: ['12 m s⁻¹', '17 m s⁻¹', '14 m s⁻¹', '21 m s⁻¹'], a: 2, e: 'Differentiate x with respect to t.', sol: ['Given: x = 3t² + 2t + 5, t = 2 s', 'Formula: v = dx/dt = 6t + 2', 'Substitution: v = 6(2) + 2', 'Answer: v = 14 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q11', p: ['iv1', 'ac3', 'pp4'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'x = 2t³ − 6t² + 4 (SI units). At the instant (t > 0) when the velocity of the particle becomes zero, its acceleration is:', o: ['Zero', '6 m s⁻²', '12 m s⁻²', '24 m s⁻²'], a: 2, e: 'Zero velocity does not mean zero acceleration.', sol: ['v = dx/dt = 6t² − 12t', 'v = 0 ⇒ 6t(t − 2) = 0 ⇒ t = 2 s (t > 0)', 'a = dv/dt = 12t − 12', 'At t = 2 s: a = 24 − 12 = 12 m s⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q12', p: ['iv2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'To find velocity at t = 4 s from Fig 2.1, NCERT takes Δt = 2 s centred at t = 4 s. The slope of line P₁P₂ then gives:', o: ['Instantaneous velocity at 4 s', 'Acceleration at 4 s', 'Average speed from 0 to 4 s', 'Average velocity over the interval 3 s to 5 s'], a: 3, e: 'Chord slope = average velocity.' },
    { id: 'q13', p: ['iv2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In the limit Δt → 0, the velocity at t = 4 s in Fig 2.1 is given by the:', o: ['Slope of chord joining t = 0 and t = 4 s', 'Area under the x–t graph up to 4 s', 'Slope of the tangent to the x–t curve at t = 4 s', 'Value x(4)/4'], a: 2, e: 'P₁P₂ becomes the tangent at P.' },
    { id: 'q14', p: ['f21'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 270 185" width="270" height="185" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38.0" y1="160.0" x2="260.0" y2="160.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="260,160 253,156.5 253,163.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="38.0" y1="160.0" x2="38.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="38,12 34.5,19 41.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="258.0" y="153.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t (s)</text><text x="44.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x (m)</text><line x1="110.0" y1="157.0" x2="110.0" y2="163.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="110.0" y="174.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">2</text><line x1="182.0" y1="157.0" x2="182.0" y2="163.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="182.0" y="174.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">4</text><line x1="254.0" y1="157.0" x2="254.0" y2="163.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="254.0" y="174.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">6</text><line x1="35.0" y1="122.0" x2="41.0" y2="122.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="126.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">5</text><line x1="35.0" y1="84.0" x2="41.0" y2="84.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="88.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">10</text><line x1="35.0" y1="46.0" x2="41.0" y2="46.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="50.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">15</text><polyline points="38.0,160.0 39.8,160.0 41.6,160.0 43.4,160.0 45.2,160.0 47.0,160.0 48.8,160.0 50.6,160.0 52.4,160.0 54.2,159.9 56.0,159.9 57.8,159.9 59.6,159.9 61.4,159.8 63.2,159.8 65.0,159.7 66.8,159.7 68.6,159.6 70.4,159.6 72.2,159.5 74.0,159.4 75.8,159.3 77.6,159.2 79.4,159.1 81.2,158.9 83.0,158.8 84.8,158.7 86.6,158.5 88.4,158.3 90.2,158.1 92.0,157.9 93.8,157.7 95.6,157.5 97.4,157.3 99.2,157.0 101.0,156.7 102.8,156.5 104.6,156.2 106.4,155.8 108.2,155.5 110.0,155.1 111.8,154.8 113.6,154.4 115.4,154.0 117.2,153.5 119.0,153.1 120.8,152.6 122.6,152.1 124.4,151.6 126.2,151.1 128.0,150.5 129.8,149.9 131.6,149.3 133.4,148.7 135.2,148.0 137.0,147.4 138.8,146.7 140.6,145.9 142.4,145.2 144.2,144.4 146.0,143.6 147.8,142.7 149.6,141.9 151.4,141.0 153.2,140.1 155.0,139.1 156.8,138.2 158.6,137.1 160.4,136.1 162.2,135.0 164.0,133.9 165.8,132.8 167.6,131.6 169.4,130.4 171.2,129.2 173.0,127.9 174.8,126.6 176.6,125.3 178.4,123.9 180.2,122.5 182.0,121.1 183.8,119.6 185.6,118.1 187.4,116.5 189.2,115.0 191.0,113.3 192.8,111.7 194.6,110.0 196.4,108.2 198.2,106.4 200.0,104.6 201.8,102.7 203.6,100.8 205.4,98.9 207.2,96.9 209.0,94.8 210.8,92.8 212.6,90.6 214.4,88.5 216.2,86.3 218.0,84.0 219.8,81.7 221.6,79.3 223.4,77.0 225.2,74.5 227.0,72.0 228.8,69.5 230.6,66.9 232.4,64.3 234.2,61.6 236.0,58.8 237.8,56.1 239.6,53.2 241.4,50.3 243.2,47.4 245.0,44.4 246.8,41.4 248.6,38.3 250.4,35.1 252.2,31.9 254.0,28.7" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="131.6,161.9 232.4,80.2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="182.0" y1="121.1" x2="182.0" y2="160.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="121.1" x2="182.0" y2="121.1" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><circle cx="182.0" cy="121.1" r="3.2" fill="currentColor"/><text x="174.0" y="115.1" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">P</text><text x="215.0" y="60.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x = 0.08 t³</text><text x="128.0" y="152.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-style="italic">tangent</text></svg>', q: 'The x–t graph shown is for x = 0.08 t³ (Fig 2.1). The slope of the tangent at P (t = 4 s) is:', o: ['1.28 m s⁻¹', '5.12 m s⁻¹', '2.56 m s⁻¹', '3.84 m s⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'Slope of tangent = instantaneous velocity.', sol: ['Given: x = 0.08 t³', 'Formula: v = dx/dt = 0.24 t²', 'Substitution: v = 0.24 × (4)² = 0.24 × 16', 'Answer: v = 3.84 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q15', p: ['f21', 't21'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For x = 0.08 t³ (Fig 2.1), the average velocity between t = 3 s and t = 5 s is:', o: ['3.84 m s⁻¹', '4.00 m s⁻¹', '7.84 m s⁻¹', '3.92 m s⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'First row of Table 2.1.', sol: ['x(5) = 0.08 × 125 = 10.0 m', 'x(3) = 0.08 × 27 = 2.16 m', 'Δx = 10.0 − 2.16 = 7.84 m, Δt = 2 s', 'Average velocity = 7.84/2 = 3.92 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q16', p: ['t21'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In Table 2.1, as Δt decreases from 2.0 s to 0.01 s, Δx/Δt:', o: ['Keeps increasing without limit', 'Approaches the limiting value 3.84 m s⁻¹', 'Becomes zero', 'Approaches 3.92 m s⁻¹'], a: 1, e: '3.92 → 3.86 → 3.845 → 3.8402 → 3.8400.' },
    { id: 'q17', p: ['t21'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In Table 2.1, for Δt = 1.0 s centred at t = 4 s, the values t₁ and t₂ are:', o: ['3.5 s and 4.5 s', '3.0 s and 5.0 s', '3.75 s and 4.25 s', '3.95 s and 4.05 s'], a: 0, e: 't₁ = t − Δt/2, t₂ = t + Δt/2.' },
    { id: 'q18', p: ['iv3'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['The graphical method for determining instantaneous velocity is always a convenient method.', 'If the exact expression for position as a function of time is known, velocity can be found using differential calculus.'], a: 3, e: 'Graphical method is not always convenient.' },
    { id: 'q19', p: ['iv3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'It is easier to calculate velocity at different instants if we have:', o: ['Only the initial position', 'Only the mass of the body', 'Data of positions at different instants or exact expression for position as a function of time', 'Only the final velocity'], a: 2, e: 'Then Δx/Δt or dx/dt can be calculated.' },
    { id: 'q20', p: ['ex21'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Position of an object is x = a + bt² with a = 8.5 m and b = 2.5 m s⁻². The average velocity between t = 2.0 s and t = 4.0 s is:', o: ['10 m s⁻¹', '20 m s⁻¹', '7.5 m s⁻¹', '15 m s⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'Example 2.1.', sol: ['x(4) = a + 16b, x(2) = a + 4b', 'Average velocity = [x(4) − x(2)]/(4.0 − 2.0) = 12b/2 = 6b', 'Substitution: 6 × 2.5', 'Answer: 15 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q21', p: ['ex21'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'For x = 8.5 + 2.5t² (SI units), the velocities at t = 0 s and t = 2.0 s are respectively:', o: ['0 and 10 m s⁻¹', '8.5 and 18.5 m s⁻¹', '0 and 5 m s⁻¹', '2.5 and 10 m s⁻¹'], a: 0, e: 'v = 2bt = 5.0t.', sol: ['v = dx/dt = 2bt = 2 × 2.5 × t = 5.0t m s⁻¹', 'At t = 0: v = 0', 'At t = 2.0 s: v = 5.0 × 2.0', 'Answer: 0 and 10 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q22', p: ['ex21', 'ke3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'For x = a + bt², the average velocity between t = 2 s and t = 4 s equals the instantaneous velocity at t = 3 s.', R: 'For constant acceleration, average velocity over an interval equals the arithmetic mean of initial and final velocities.' }, a: 0, e: 'v(2) = 10, v(4) = 20, mean 15 = v(3); a = 2b is constant.' },
    { id: 'q23', p: ['iv4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For uniform motion, the instantaneous velocity is:', o: ['Zero at all instants', 'Increasing with time', 'Equal to the acceleration', 'The same as the average velocity at all instants'], a: 3, e: 'NCERT 2.2.' },
    { id: 'q24', p: ['iv4', 'su2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['For uniform motion, instantaneous velocity equals average velocity at all instants.', 'For uniform motion, the x–t graph is a parabola.'], a: 2, e: 'x–t is a straight line inclined to time axis.' },
    { id: 'q25', p: ['sp1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Velocities of +24.0 m s⁻¹ and −24.0 m s⁻¹ have speeds:', o: ['+24 and −24 m s⁻¹', '0 and 24 m s⁻¹', '24.0 m s⁻¹ each', '48 m s⁻¹ each'], a: 2, e: 'Speed is magnitude of velocity.' },
    { id: 'q26', p: ['sp1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Average speed over a finite interval is greater than or equal to the magnitude of average velocity.', R: 'Instantaneous speed is equal to the magnitude of instantaneous velocity at that instant.' }, a: 1, e: 'A follows from path length ≥ |displacement|, not from R.' },
    { id: 'q27', p: ['sp1', 'su1', 'tq1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A particle moves 30 m along +x in 10 s and then 10 m back along −x in the next 10 s. Its average speed and magnitude of average velocity for the whole motion are:', o: ['2 m s⁻¹ and 1 m s⁻¹', '1 m s⁻¹ and 2 m s⁻¹', '2 m s⁻¹ and 2 m s⁻¹', '4 m s⁻¹ and 1 m s⁻¹'], a: 0, e: 'Path length ≥ |displacement|.', sol: ['Path length = 30 + 10 = 40 m; displacement = 30 − 10 = 20 m; time = 20 s', 'Average speed = path length / time = 40/20 = 2 m s⁻¹', '|Average velocity| = displacement / time = 20/20 = 1 m s⁻¹', 'Answer: 2 m s⁻¹ and 1 m s⁻¹'] },

    { id: 'q28', p: ['ac1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'From studies of free fall and inclined plane, Galileo concluded that for all objects in free fall the following is constant:', o: ['Rate of change of velocity with time', 'Rate of change of velocity with distance', 'Velocity', 'Distance covered per second'], a: 0, e: 'This led to the concept of acceleration.' },
    { id: 'q29', p: ['ac1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['During free fall, change in velocity with distance is constant.', 'Change in velocity with distance decreases with increasing distance of fall.'], a: 3, e: 'With distance it is not constant.' },
    { id: 'q30', p: ['ac2'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'Velocity of a body changes from 10 m s⁻¹ to 30 m s⁻¹ in 4 s. Its average acceleration is:', o: ['2.5 m s⁻²', '10 m s⁻²', '5 m s⁻²', '20 m s⁻²'], a: 2, e: 'ā = Δv/Δt.', sol: ['Given: v₁ = 10 m s⁻¹, v₂ = 30 m s⁻¹, Δt = 4 s', 'Formula: ā = (v₂ − v₁)/(t₂ − t₁)', 'Substitution: ā = (30 − 10)/4 = 20/4', 'Answer: 5 m s⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q31', p: ['ac2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'On a v–t plot, the average acceleration between points (v₁, t₁) and (v₂, t₂) is:', o: ['Area under the curve', 'Slope of the straight line connecting the two points', 'Slope of tangent at t₁', 'Product v × t'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT 2.3.' },
    { id: 'q32', p: ['ac2', 'tq2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'SI unit and dimension of acceleration are:', o: ['m s⁻¹, [LT⁻¹]', 'm² s⁻¹, [L²T⁻¹]', 'm s, [LT]', 'm s⁻², [LT⁻²]'], a: 3, e: 'm s⁻² and [LT⁻²].' },
    { id: 'q33', p: ['ac3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Velocity of a particle is v = 4t² − 2t (SI units). Its acceleration at t = 3 s is:', o: ['22 m s⁻²', '30 m s⁻²', '24 m s⁻²', '16 m s⁻²'], a: 0, e: 'a = dv/dt.', sol: ['Given: v = 4t² − 2t', 'Formula: a = dv/dt = 8t − 2', 'Substitution: a = 8(3) − 2', 'Answer: 22 m s⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q34', p: ['ac3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The acceleration of an object at an instant is:', o: ['Area under the v–t curve', 'Slope of the chord of x–t curve', 'Slope of the tangent to the v–t curve at that instant', 'Always zero'], a: 2, e: 'a = dv/dt.' },
    { id: 'q35', p: ['ac4'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Acceleration of a body may result from:', s: ['A change in speed.', 'A change in direction of velocity.', 'A change in both speed and direction.', 'A change in position at constant velocity.'], o: ['I and II only', 'I, II and III only', 'II, III and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 1, e: 'At constant velocity acceleration is zero.' },
    { id: 'q36', p: ['ac4'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Like velocity, acceleration can be positive, negative or zero.', 'Since velocity has magnitude and direction, a change in velocity may involve either or both of these.'], a: 0, e: 'Both NCERT statements.' },
    { id: 'q37', p: ['f22'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 290 128" width="290" height="128" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="20.0" y1="100.0" x2="94.0" y2="100.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="94,100 87,96.5 87,103.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="20.0" y1="100.0" x2="20.0" y2="16.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="20,16 16.5,23 23.5,23" fill="currentColor"/><text x="26.0" y="24.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x</text><text x="90.0" y="114.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t</text><polyline points="28.0,53.4 29.9,55.5 31.9,57.4 33.8,59.2 35.7,60.8 37.7,62.2 39.6,63.5 41.5,64.6 43.5,65.6 45.4,66.4 47.3,67.0 49.3,67.5 51.2,67.8 53.1,68.0 55.1,68.0 57.0,67.8 58.9,67.5 60.9,67.0 62.8,66.4 64.7,65.6 66.7,64.6 68.6,63.5 70.5,62.2 72.5,60.8 74.4,59.2 76.3,57.4 78.3,55.5 80.2,53.4 82.1,51.2 84.1,48.8 86.0,46.2" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="57.0" y="122.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">(A)</text><line x1="112.0" y1="100.0" x2="186.0" y2="100.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="186,100 179,96.5 179,103.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="112.0" y1="100.0" x2="112.0" y2="16.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="112,16 108.5,23 115.5,23" fill="currentColor"/><text x="118.0" y="24.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x</text><text x="182.0" y="114.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t</text><polyline points="120.0,56.0 121.9,53.7 123.9,51.5 125.8,49.5 127.7,47.7 129.7,46.0 131.6,44.5 133.5,43.1 135.5,41.9 137.4,40.9 139.3,40.0 141.3,39.3 143.2,38.7 145.1,38.3 147.1,38.1 149.0,38.0 150.9,38.1 152.9,38.3 154.8,38.7 156.7,39.3 158.7,40.0 160.6,40.9 162.5,41.9 164.5,43.1 166.4,44.5 168.3,46.0 170.3,47.7 172.2,49.5 174.1,51.5 176.1,53.7 178.0,56.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="149.0" y="122.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">(B)</text><line x1="204.0" y1="100.0" x2="278.0" y2="100.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="278,100 271,96.5 271,103.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="204.0" y1="100.0" x2="204.0" y2="16.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="204,16 200.5,23 207.5,23" fill="currentColor"/><text x="210.0" y="24.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x</text><text x="274.0" y="114.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t</text><polyline points="212.0,90.0 213.9,87.9 215.9,85.9 217.8,83.8 219.7,81.7 221.7,79.7 223.6,77.6 225.5,75.5 227.5,73.5 229.4,71.4 231.3,69.3 233.3,67.3 235.2,65.2 237.1,63.1 239.1,61.1 241.0,59.0 242.9,56.9 244.9,54.9 246.8,52.8 248.7,50.7 250.7,48.7 252.6,46.6 254.5,44.5 256.5,42.5 258.4,40.4 260.3,38.3 262.3,36.3 264.2,34.2 266.1,32.1 268.1,30.1 270.0,28.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="241.0" y="122.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">(C)</text></svg>', q: 'Match the x–t graphs A, B, C shown with the type of acceleration.', match: { h: ['x–t graph', 'Acceleration'], l: ['Graph A (curves upward)', 'Graph B (curves downward)', 'Graph C (straight line)'], r: ['Zero acceleration', 'Positive acceleration', 'Negative acceleration'] }, o: ['A-II, B-III, C-I', 'A-III, B-II, C-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II', 'A-II, B-I, C-III'], a: 0, e: 'Fig 2.2.' },
    { id: 'q38', p: ['f22'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For zero acceleration, the position–time graph is:', o: ['Curving upward', 'Curving downward', 'A circle', 'A straight line'], a: 3, e: 'Fig 2.2(c).' },
    { id: 'q39', p: ['ac5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For constant acceleration a, if velocity is v₀ at t = 0, the velocity at time t is:', o: ['v = v₀ − at', 'v = v₀ + ½at²', 'v = at²', 'v = v₀ + at'], a: 3, e: 'Eq. 2.4.' },
    { id: 'q40', p: ['ac5', 'ke5'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'For motion with constant acceleration, average acceleration over any interval equals that constant acceleration.', R: 'Kinematic equations connect five quantities v₀, v, a, t and x.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true but R does not explain A.' },
    { id: 'q41', p: ['f23'], type: 'match', lvl: 3, q: 'Match the velocity–time graphs of Fig 2.3 with the motion.', match: { h: ['Graph', 'Motion'], l: ['Fig 2.3(a)', 'Fig 2.3(b)', 'Fig 2.3(c)', 'Fig 2.3(d)'], r: ['Negative direction with negative acceleration', 'Positive direction with negative acceleration', 'Negative acceleration, changes direction at t₁', 'Positive direction with positive acceleration'] }, o: ['A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III', 'A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III', 'A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III', 'A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV'], a: 2, e: 'Fig 2.3 caption.' },
    { id: 'q42', p: ['f23', 'ac6'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 250 190" width="250" height="190" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="40.0" y1="95.0" x2="240.0" y2="95.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="240,95 233,91.5 233,98.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="40.0" y1="180.0" x2="40.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,12 36.5,19 43.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="238.0" y="88.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t (s)</text><text x="46.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v (m s⁻¹)</text><line x1="128.0" y1="92.0" x2="128.0" y2="98.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="128.0" y="109.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">2</text><line x1="216.0" y1="92.0" x2="216.0" y2="98.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="216.0" y="109.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">4</text><line x1="37.0" y1="30.0" x2="43.0" y2="30.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="34.0" y="34.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">10</text><line x1="37.0" y1="160.0" x2="43.0" y2="160.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="34.0" y="164.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">−10</text><polyline points="40.0,30.0 216.0,160.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="216.0" y1="160.0" x2="216.0" y2="95.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="40.0" y1="160.0" x2="216.0" y2="160.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'The v–t graph of a particle is shown (like Fig 2.3d). Its displacement and distance travelled in 0–4 s are:', o: ['20 m and 20 m', '0 and 20 m', '0 and 0', '10 m and 20 m'], a: 1, e: 'Particle turns back at t = 2 s.', sol: ['Area from 0 to 2 s = ½ × 2 × 10 = +10 m (moving in +x)', 'Area from 2 to 4 s = ½ × 2 × (−10) = −10 m (moving back)', 'Displacement = 10 + (−10) = 0', 'Distance = |10| + |−10| = 20 m'] },
    { id: 'q43', p: ['ac6'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 270 170" width="270" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38.0" y1="145.0" x2="260.0" y2="145.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="260,145 253,141.5 253,148.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="38.0" y1="145.0" x2="38.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="38,12 34.5,19 41.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="258.0" y="138.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t (s)</text><text x="44.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v (m s⁻¹)</text><line x1="118.0" y1="142.0" x2="118.0" y2="148.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="118.0" y="159.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">4</text><line x1="198.0" y1="142.0" x2="198.0" y2="148.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="198.0" y="159.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">8</text><line x1="238.0" y1="142.0" x2="238.0" y2="148.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="238.0" y="159.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">10</text><line x1="35.0" y1="45.0" x2="41.0" y2="45.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="49.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">20</text><polyline points="38.0,145.0 118.0,45.0 198.0,45.0 238.0,145.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="118.0" y1="45.0" x2="118.0" y2="145.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="45.0" x2="118.0" y2="45.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="198.0" y1="45.0" x2="198.0" y2="145.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="45.0" x2="198.0" y2="45.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'The v–t graph of a car is shown. The displacement of the car in 10 s is:', o: ['120 m', '160 m', '200 m', '140 m'], a: 3, e: 'Area under v–t curve = displacement.', sol: ['0–4 s (triangle): ½ × 4 × 20 = 40 m', '4–8 s (rectangle): 4 × 20 = 80 m', '8–10 s (triangle): ½ × 2 × 20 = 20 m', 'Total displacement = 40 + 80 + 20 = 140 m'] },
    { id: 'q44', p: ['ac6', 'ac2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 270 170" width="270" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38.0" y1="145.0" x2="260.0" y2="145.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="260,145 253,141.5 253,148.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="38.0" y1="145.0" x2="38.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="38,12 34.5,19 41.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="258.0" y="138.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t (s)</text><text x="44.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v (m s⁻¹)</text><line x1="118.0" y1="142.0" x2="118.0" y2="148.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="118.0" y="159.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">4</text><line x1="198.0" y1="142.0" x2="198.0" y2="148.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="198.0" y="159.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">8</text><line x1="238.0" y1="142.0" x2="238.0" y2="148.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="238.0" y="159.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">10</text><line x1="35.0" y1="45.0" x2="41.0" y2="45.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="49.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">20</text><polyline points="38.0,145.0 118.0,45.0 198.0,45.0 238.0,145.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="118.0" y1="45.0" x2="118.0" y2="145.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="45.0" x2="118.0" y2="45.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="198.0" y1="45.0" x2="198.0" y2="145.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="45.0" x2="198.0" y2="45.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'For the same v–t graph, the acceleration in the last 2 s and the average velocity over 10 s are:', o: ['−10 m s⁻² and 14 m s⁻¹', '−10 m s⁻² and 20 m s⁻¹', '+10 m s⁻² and 14 m s⁻¹', '−5 m s⁻² and 10 m s⁻¹'], a: 0, e: 'Slope gives acceleration; area gives displacement.', sol: ['Slope 8–10 s: a = (0 − 20)/(10 − 8) = −10 m s⁻²', 'Displacement = area = 140 m', 'Average velocity = 140/10', 'Answer: −10 m s⁻² and 14 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q45', p: ['ac6'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For an object moving with constant velocity u, the area under its v–t curve from t = 0 to t = T is:', o: ['u/T', 'uT, which is the displacement', 'u + T', 'Zero'], a: 1, e: 'Fig 2.4.' },
    { id: 'q46', p: ['kk1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In realistic situations, x–t, v–t and a–t graphs have sharp kinks.', 'Velocity of a real object can change its value abruptly at an instant.'], a: 1, e: 'Realistic graphs are smooth; changes are always continuous.' },
    { id: 'q47', p: ['kk1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Sharp kinks in x–t or v–t graphs imply that:', o: ['The functions are not differentiable at those points', 'Velocity is infinite there always', 'Motion is uniform', 'Acceleration is zero everywhere'], a: 0, e: 'NCERT note.' },

    { id: 'q48', p: ['ke1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In Fig 2.5, the area under the v–t curve between 0 and t (area of triangle ABC + rectangle OACD) equals:', o: ['(v − v₀)t + ½v₀t', '½(v − v₀)t + v₀t', '½vt only', 'v₀t − ½(v − v₀)t'], a: 1, e: 'This area is the displacement x.' },
    { id: 'q49', p: ['ke1', 'ac6'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 240 175" width="240" height="175" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38.0" y1="150.0" x2="230.0" y2="150.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="230,150 223,146.5 223,153.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="38.0" y1="150.0" x2="38.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="38,12 34.5,19 41.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="228.0" y="143.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t (s)</text><text x="44.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v (m s⁻¹)</text><line x1="182.0" y1="147.0" x2="182.0" y2="153.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="182.0" y="164.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">4</text><line x1="35.0" y1="127.0" x2="41.0" y2="127.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="131.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">5</text><line x1="35.0" y1="35.0" x2="41.0" y2="35.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="39.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">25</text><polyline points="38.0,127.0 182.0,35.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="182.0" y1="35.0" x2="182.0" y2="150.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="35.0" x2="182.0" y2="35.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'The v–t graph of a body moving with uniform acceleration is shown. Its displacement in 4 s is:', o: ['60 m', '100 m', '40 m', '80 m'], a: 0, e: 'Triangle + rectangle (Fig 2.5).', sol: ['Rectangle: v₀ × t = 5 × 4 = 20 m', 'Triangle: ½ (v − v₀) t = ½ × 20 × 4 = 40 m', 'Displacement = 20 + 40', 'Answer: 60 m'] },
    { id: 'q50', p: ['ke1', 'ac2'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 240 175" width="240" height="175" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38.0" y1="150.0" x2="230.0" y2="150.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="230,150 223,146.5 223,153.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="38.0" y1="150.0" x2="38.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="38,12 34.5,19 41.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="228.0" y="143.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t (s)</text><text x="44.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v (m s⁻¹)</text><line x1="182.0" y1="147.0" x2="182.0" y2="153.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="182.0" y="164.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">4</text><line x1="35.0" y1="127.0" x2="41.0" y2="127.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="131.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">5</text><line x1="35.0" y1="35.0" x2="41.0" y2="35.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="39.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">25</text><polyline points="38.0,127.0 182.0,35.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="182.0" y1="35.0" x2="182.0" y2="150.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="35.0" x2="182.0" y2="35.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'For the same v–t graph, the acceleration of the body is:', o: ['4 m s⁻²', '6.25 m s⁻²', '5 m s⁻²', '20 m s⁻²'], a: 2, e: 'Slope of v–t line.', sol: ['Formula: a = (v − v₀)/t', 'Substitution: a = (25 − 5)/4', 'a = 20/4', 'Answer: 5 m s⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q51', p: ['ke2'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'A car moving at 10 m s⁻¹ accelerates uniformly at 2 m s⁻² for 5 s. Distance covered in this time is:', o: ['50 m', '100 m', '25 m', '75 m'], a: 3, e: 'x = v₀t + ½at².', sol: ['Given: v₀ = 10 m s⁻¹, a = 2 m s⁻², t = 5 s', 'Formula: x = v₀t + ½at²', 'Substitution: x = 10 × 5 + ½ × 2 × 25 = 50 + 25', 'Answer: 75 m'] },
    { id: 'q52', p: ['ke2', 'gl1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A body starting from rest with uniform acceleration covers 20 m in the first 2 s. The distance covered in the next 2 s is:', o: ['20 m', '40 m', '60 m', '80 m'], a: 2, e: 'Distances in equal intervals from rest are in ratio 1 : 3.', sol: ['x = ½at² ⇒ 20 = ½ × a × 4 ⇒ a = 10 m s⁻²', 'Distance in 4 s: x = ½ × 10 × 16 = 80 m', 'Distance in next 2 s = 80 − 20', 'Answer: 60 m (ratio 20 : 60 = 1 : 3)'] },
    { id: 'q53', p: ['ke2', 'iv4'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'Car A starts from rest with acceleration 2 m s⁻² at the instant car B passes it with a constant velocity of 10 m s⁻¹ in the same direction. A catches B after:', o: ['5 s', '10 s', '20 s', '2.5 s'], a: 1, e: 'Equal displacements at the meeting instant.', sol: ['Car A: x_A = ½ × 2 × t² = t²', 'Car B (uniform motion): x_B = 10t', 'Meet when t² = 10t ⇒ t = 10 s', 'They meet 100 m ahead after 10 s'] },
    { id: 'q54', p: ['ke3'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'A body with uniform acceleration changes its velocity from 4 m s⁻¹ to 16 m s⁻¹ in 6 s. Distance covered is:', o: ['60 m', '48 m', '72 m', '96 m'], a: 0, e: 'x = [(v + v₀)/2] t.', sol: ['Given: v₀ = 4 m s⁻¹, v = 16 m s⁻¹, t = 6 s', 'Formula: x = v̄t = [(v + v₀)/2] t', 'Substitution: x = [(16 + 4)/2] × 6 = 10 × 6', 'Answer: 60 m'] },
    { id: 'q55', p: ['ke3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The relation v̄ = (v + v₀)/2 for average velocity holds:', o: ['For all types of motion', 'Only when velocity is zero', 'For constant acceleration only', 'Only for circular motion'], a: 2, e: 'Eq. 2.7b.' },
    { id: 'q56', p: ['ke3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'For uniformly accelerated motion, x = (v + v₀)t/2.', R: 'For constant acceleration, average velocity is (v − v₀)/2.' }, a: 2, e: 'Average velocity is (v + v₀)/2.' },
    { id: 'q57', p: ['ke4', 'xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A car moving along a straight highway at 126 km h⁻¹ is brought to a stop within 200 m. Its retardation (assumed uniform) and the time it takes to stop are:', o: ['6.12 m s⁻² and 5.7 s', '3.06 m s⁻² and 5.7 s', '3.06 m s⁻² and 11.4 s', '1.53 m s⁻² and 22.8 s'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 2.5.', sol: ['Unit conversion: 126 km h⁻¹ = 126 × 5/18 = 35 m s⁻¹', 'v² = v₀² + 2ax ⇒ 0 = 35² − 2a(200) ⇒ a = 1225/400 ≈ 3.06 m s⁻²', 'v = v₀ − at ⇒ t = 35/3.06', 'Answer: ≈ 3.06 m s⁻² and ≈ 11.4 s'] },
    { id: 'q58', p: ['ke4'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A bullet moving at 200 m s⁻¹ penetrates 10 cm into a wooden block and stops. Assuming uniform retardation, its magnitude is:', o: ['2 × 10⁴ m s⁻²', '4 × 10⁵ m s⁻²', '1 × 10⁵ m s⁻²', '2 × 10⁵ m s⁻²'], a: 3, e: 'Use v² = v₀² + 2ax with cm → m.', sol: ['Given: v₀ = 200 m s⁻¹, v = 0, x = 10 cm = 0.1 m', 'Formula: 0 = v₀² − 2ax ⇒ a = v₀²/2x', 'Substitution: a = (200)²/(2 × 0.1) = 40000/0.2', 'Answer: 2 × 10⁵ m s⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q59', p: ['ke4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In NCERT, v² = v₀² + 2ax is obtained by:', o: ['Substituting t = (v − v₀)/a into x = [(v + v₀)/2] t', 'Differentiating x = v₀t', 'Putting a = 0 in v = v₀ + at', 'Integrating x with respect to v'], a: 0, e: 'Eq. 2.8.' },
    { id: 'q60', p: ['ke5'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A particle starts at x₀ = 5 m with v₀ = 3 m s⁻¹ and constant acceleration 2 m s⁻² along +x. Its position at t = 4 s is:', o: ['28 m', '21 m', '17 m', '33 m'], a: 3, e: 'x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at².', sol: ['Formula: x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at² (Eq. 2.9b)', 'Substitution: x = 5 + 3 × 4 + ½ × 2 × 16', 'x = 5 + 12 + 16', 'Answer: 33 m'] },
    { id: 'q61', p: ['ke5'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The equations v = v₀ + at, x = v₀t + ½at², v² = v₀² + 2ax connect five quantities v₀, v, a, t and x.', 'These equations assume x = 0 at t = 0; otherwise x is replaced by (x − x₀).'], a: 0, e: 'Eq. 2.9a–2.9c.' },
    { id: 'q62', p: ['ex22'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'To derive v² − v₀² = 2a(x − x₀) by calculus, NCERT writes acceleration as:', o: ['a = dx/dt', 'a = x dv/dt', 'a = v dv/dx', 'a = v dt/dx'], a: 2, e: 'a = (dv/dx)(dx/dt) = v dv/dx.' },
    { id: 'q63', p: ['ex22', 'pp6'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'The calculus method of Example 2.2 can be used for motion with non-uniform acceleration also.', R: 'The kinematic equations (2.9) are true only when acceleration is constant.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R is about Eq. 2.9, not the reason for A.' },
    { id: 'q64', p: ['ex23a', 'f26'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A ball is thrown vertically upwards with 20 m s⁻¹ from the top of a building 25.0 m high (g = 10 m s⁻²). Maximum height of the ball above the ground is:', o: ['20 m', '25 m', '45 m', '40 m'], a: 2, e: 'Example 2.3.', sol: ['Upward positive: v₀ = +20 m s⁻¹, a = −10 m s⁻², v = 0 at top', 'v² = v₀² + 2a(y − y₀) ⇒ 0 = 400 − 20(y − y₀)', 'y − y₀ = 20 m above the launch point', 'Height above ground = 25 + 20 = 45 m'] },
    { id: 'q65', p: ['ex23a', 'ex23b'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'In Example 2.3 (v₀ = 20 m s⁻¹ upward, g = 10 m s⁻²), the time taken to reach the highest point is:', o: ['1 s', '2 s', '3 s', '4 s'], a: 1, e: 'v = v₀ + at.', sol: ['At the highest point v = 0', 'v = v₀ + at ⇒ 0 = 20 − 10t₁', 't₁ = 20/10', 'Answer: 2 s'] },
    { id: 'q66', p: ['ex23b'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'In Example 2.3 (thrown up at 20 m s⁻¹ from 25 m high building, g = 10 m s⁻²), the ball hits the ground after:', o: ['3 s', '4 s', '5 s', '6 s'], a: 2, e: 'Use y = y₀ + v₀t + ½at² for the whole motion.', sol: ['y₀ = 25 m, y = 0, v₀ = +20 m s⁻¹, a = −10 m s⁻²', '0 = 25 + 20t − 5t² ⇒ t² − 4t − 5 = 0', '(t − 5)(t + 1) = 0 ⇒ t = 5 s (t = −1 rejected)', 'Check: up 2 s + down from 45 m 3 s = 5 s'] },
    { id: 'q67', p: ['ex23b'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Using y = y₀ + v₀t + ½at² for the whole flight is a better method than splitting the motion into upward and downward parts.', R: 'The motion is under constant acceleration, so we need not worry about the path.' }, a: 0, e: 'NCERT says the second method is better for this reason.' },
    { id: 'q68', p: ['ex23b', 'ke2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A ball is thrown vertically up with 30 m s⁻¹ (g = 10 m s⁻²). It is at a height of 25 m above the point of projection at times:', o: ['2 s and 4 s', '1 s and 5 s', '1 s and 6 s', '0.5 s and 5.5 s'], a: 1, e: 'Once going up, once coming down.', sol: ['y = v₀t + ½at² ⇒ 25 = 30t − 5t²', '5t² − 30t + 25 = 0 ⇒ t² − 6t + 5 = 0', '(t − 1)(t − 5) = 0', 'Answer: t = 1 s (going up) and t = 5 s (coming down)'] },
    { id: 'q69', p: ['f26'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In Fig 2.6 (Example 2.3), the origin and the axis are chosen as:', o: ['Origin at top of building, y downward', 'Origin at the ground, y-axis vertically upward', 'Origin at highest point B', 'Origin at A, axis horizontal'], a: 1, e: 'So a = −10 m s⁻².' },
    { id: 'q70', p: ['f26', 'ke4'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 250 192" width="250" height="192" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10.0" y1="175.0" x2="240.0" y2="175.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="14.0" y1="175.0" x2="7.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="26.0" y1="175.0" x2="19.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="38.0" y1="175.0" x2="31.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="50.0" y1="175.0" x2="43.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="62.0" y1="175.0" x2="55.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="74.0" y1="175.0" x2="67.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="86.0" y1="175.0" x2="79.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="98.0" y1="175.0" x2="91.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="110.0" y1="175.0" x2="103.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="122.0" y1="175.0" x2="115.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="134.0" y1="175.0" x2="127.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="146.0" y1="175.0" x2="139.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="158.0" y1="175.0" x2="151.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="170.0" y1="175.0" x2="163.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="182.0" y1="175.0" x2="175.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="194.0" y1="175.0" x2="187.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="206.0" y1="175.0" x2="199.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="218.0" y1="175.0" x2="211.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="230.0" y1="175.0" x2="223.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="60" y="55" width="46" height="120" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="70" y="69" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="87" y="69" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="70" y="91" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="87" y="91" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="70" y="113" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="87" y="113" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="70" y="135" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="87" y="135" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="70" y="157" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="87" y="157" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><circle cx="118" cy="48" r="6" fill="#D9A520"/><line x1="118.0" y1="39.0" x2="118.0" y2="9.0" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.2"/><polygon points="118,3 113.5,12 122.5,12" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="128.0" y="25.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">20 m s⁻¹</text><line x1="34.0" y1="55.0" x2="34.0" y2="175.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="34,55 30.5,62 37.5,62" fill="currentColor"/><polygon points="34,175 30.5,168 37.5,168" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="28.0" y1="55.0" x2="60.0" y2="55.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="30.0" y="119.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">60 m</text><text x="188.0" y="105.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">g = 10 m s⁻²</text><line x1="188.0" y1="115.0" x2="188.0" y2="145.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><polygon points="188,149 184.5,142 191.5,142" fill="currentColor"/></svg>', q: 'A ball is thrown vertically upward with 20 m s⁻¹ from the top of a 60 m high tower as shown (g = 10 m s⁻²). The speed with which it hits the ground is:', o: ['20 m s⁻¹', '40 m s⁻¹', '30 m s⁻¹', '60 m s⁻¹'], a: 1, e: 'Only initial and final positions matter.', sol: ['Upward positive: v₀ = +20 m s⁻¹, a = −10 m s⁻², y − y₀ = 0 − 60 = −60 m', 'v² = v₀² + 2a(y − y₀) = 400 + 2(−10)(−60)', 'v² = 400 + 1200 = 1600', 'Answer: speed = 40 m s⁻¹ (downward)'] },
    { id: 'q71', p: ['f26', 'ex23b'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 250 192" width="250" height="192" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="10.0" y1="175.0" x2="240.0" y2="175.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="14.0" y1="175.0" x2="7.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="26.0" y1="175.0" x2="19.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="38.0" y1="175.0" x2="31.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="50.0" y1="175.0" x2="43.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="62.0" y1="175.0" x2="55.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="74.0" y1="175.0" x2="67.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="86.0" y1="175.0" x2="79.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="98.0" y1="175.0" x2="91.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="110.0" y1="175.0" x2="103.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="122.0" y1="175.0" x2="115.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="134.0" y1="175.0" x2="127.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="146.0" y1="175.0" x2="139.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="158.0" y1="175.0" x2="151.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="170.0" y1="175.0" x2="163.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="182.0" y1="175.0" x2="175.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="194.0" y1="175.0" x2="187.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="206.0" y1="175.0" x2="199.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="218.0" y1="175.0" x2="211.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="230.0" y1="175.0" x2="223.0" y2="183.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="60" y="55" width="46" height="120" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="70" y="69" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="87" y="69" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="70" y="91" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="87" y="91" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="70" y="113" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="87" y="113" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="70" y="135" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="87" y="135" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="70" y="157" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="87" y="157" width="9" height="11" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><circle cx="118" cy="48" r="6" fill="#D9A520"/><line x1="118.0" y1="39.0" x2="118.0" y2="9.0" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.2"/><polygon points="118,3 113.5,12 122.5,12" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="128.0" y="25.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">20 m s⁻¹</text><line x1="34.0" y1="55.0" x2="34.0" y2="175.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="34,55 30.5,62 37.5,62" fill="currentColor"/><polygon points="34,175 30.5,168 37.5,168" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="28.0" y1="55.0" x2="60.0" y2="55.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="30.0" y="119.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">60 m</text><text x="188.0" y="105.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">g = 10 m s⁻²</text><line x1="188.0" y1="115.0" x2="188.0" y2="145.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><polygon points="188,149 184.5,142 191.5,142" fill="currentColor"/></svg>', q: 'For the same ball (20 m s⁻¹ upward from a 60 m tower, g = 10 m s⁻²), the time after which it hits the ground is:', o: ['4 s', '5 s', '2 s', '6 s'], a: 3, e: 'Second method of Example 2.3.', sol: ['y = y₀ + v₀t + ½at² ⇒ 0 = 60 + 20t − 5t²', 't² − 4t − 12 = 0', '(t − 6)(t + 2) = 0', 'Answer: t = 6 s'] },
    { id: 'q72', p: ['ff1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'An object is said to be in free fall when:', o: ['It moves under gravity with air resistance neglected', 'It moves with constant velocity', 'It slides on an inclined plane', 'It moves in a circle'], a: 0, e: 'Example 2.4.' },
    { id: 'q73', p: ['ff1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['g can be taken constant if the height of fall is small compared to the earth\'s radius.', 'Free fall is a case of motion with non-uniform acceleration.'], a: 2, e: 'Free fall is uniformly accelerated motion.' },
    { id: 'q74', p: ['ff2'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'An object is released from rest (g = 9.8 m s⁻²). After 3 s, the distance fallen and its speed are:', o: ['29.4 m and 44.1 m s⁻¹', '44.1 m and 29.4 m s⁻¹', '88.2 m and 29.4 m s⁻¹', '44.1 m and 14.7 m s⁻¹'], a: 1, e: 'y = −4.9t², v = −9.8t.', sol: ['Distance: |y| = ½gt² = 4.9 × 3² = 4.9 × 9 = 44.1 m', 'Speed: |v| = gt = 9.8 × 3', '|v| = 29.4 m s⁻¹', 'Answer: 44.1 m and 29.4 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q75', p: ['ff2'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'Using v² = −19.6y for free fall from rest (upward positive), the speed after falling 10 m is:', o: ['14 m s⁻¹', '9.8 m s⁻¹', '19.6 m s⁻¹', '196 m s⁻¹'], a: 0, e: 'y = −10 m.', sol: ['After falling 10 m, y = −10 m', 'v² = −19.6 × (−10) = 196 m² s⁻²', 'v = √196', 'Answer: 14 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q76', p: ['ff2', 'pp3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Choosing upward direction as positive, the acceleration of a freely falling body is:', o: ['+9.8 m s⁻²', 'Zero', 'Depends on its mass', '−9.8 m s⁻²'], a: 3, e: 'a = −g.' },
    { id: 'q77', p: ['f27'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 250 198" width="250" height="198" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="46.0" y1="26.0" x2="236.0" y2="26.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="236,26 229,22.5 229,29.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="46.0" y1="26.0" x2="46.0" y2="190.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="46,190 42.5,183 49.5,183" fill="currentColor"/><text x="236.0" y="44.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t (s)</text><text x="54.0" y="186.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">v (m s⁻¹)</text><line x1="80.0" y1="23.0" x2="80.0" y2="29.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="80.0" y="19.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">1</text><line x1="114.0" y1="23.0" x2="114.0" y2="29.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="114.0" y="19.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">2</text><line x1="148.0" y1="23.0" x2="148.0" y2="29.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="148.0" y="19.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">3</text><line x1="182.0" y1="23.0" x2="182.0" y2="29.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="182.0" y="19.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">4</text><line x1="216.0" y1="23.0" x2="216.0" y2="29.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="216.0" y="19.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">5</text><line x1="43.0" y1="82.0" x2="49.0" y2="82.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="40.0" y="86.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">−20</text><line x1="43.0" y1="138.0" x2="49.0" y2="138.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="40.0" y="142.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">−40</text><text x="40.0" y="30.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">0</text><polyline points="46.0,26.0 216.0,163.2" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="216.0" y1="163.2" x2="216.0" y2="26.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="46.0" y1="163.2" x2="216.0" y2="163.2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="40.0" y="167.2" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">−49</text></svg>', q: 'The v–t graph of an object in free fall (Fig 2.7b) is shown. The slope of this graph is:', o: ['−9.8 m s⁻²', '+9.8 m s⁻²', '−49 m s⁻²', '−4.9 m s⁻²'], a: 0, e: 'Slope of v–t = acceleration.', sol: ['From graph: v changes from 0 to −49 m s⁻¹ in 5 s', 'Slope = Δv/Δt = (−49 − 0)/(5 − 0)', 'Slope = −9.8 m s⁻²', 'This is a = −g'] },
    { id: 'q78', p: ['f27'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the graph of an object in free fall (Fig 2.7) with its shape.', match: { h: ['Graph', 'Shape'], l: ['Acceleration–time', 'Velocity–time', 'Distance (y)–time'], r: ['Parabola curving downward', 'Straight line parallel to time axis at −9.8 m s⁻²', 'Straight line through origin with negative slope'] }, o: ['A-I, B-III, C-II', 'A-II, B-III, C-I', 'A-III, B-II, C-I', 'A-II, B-I, C-III'], a: 1, e: 'Fig 2.7(a), (b), (c).' },
    { id: 'q79', p: ['gl1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Galileo\'s law of odd numbers: distances fallen from rest in successive equal intervals are in the ratio:', o: ['1 : 2 : 3 : 4', '1 : 4 : 9 : 16', '1 : 1 : 1 : 1', '1 : 3 : 5 : 7'], a: 3, e: 'Example 2.5.' },
    { id: 'q80', p: ['gl1', 'ff2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A body is dropped from rest (g = 10 m s⁻²). The distance it falls in the 5th second is:', o: ['50 m', '125 m', '45 m', '80 m'], a: 2, e: 'Distance in 5th second = x(5) − x(4).', sol: ['x(t) = ½gt² = 5t²', 'x(5) = 125 m, x(4) = 80 m', 'Distance in 5th second = 125 − 80', 'Answer: 45 m (= 9 × 5 m, 9th odd ratio term)'] },
    { id: 'q81', p: ['gl1', 'ff2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A body falling freely from rest covers in its last second the same distance that it covered in the first 3 s (g = 10 m s⁻²). The height from which it fell is:', o: ['125 m', '100 m', '80 m', '180 m'], a: 0, e: 'Multi-step free fall.', sol: ['Distance in first 3 s = ½ × 10 × 9 = 45 m', 'If total time is t: distance in last second = 5t² − 5(t − 1)² = 10t − 5', '10t − 5 = 45 ⇒ t = 5 s', 'Height h = ½ × 10 × 25 = 125 m'] },
    { id: 'q82', p: ['gl1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Galileo Galilei (1564–1642) was the first to:', o: ['Discover the law of gravitation', 'Invent calculus', 'Measure the speed of light', 'Make quantitative studies of free fall'], a: 3, e: 'Example 2.5.' },
    { id: 'q83', p: ['t22'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In Table 2.2 (y₀ = −½gτ²), the position after 4τ and the distance traversed in the 4th interval are:', o: ['16y₀ and 9y₀', '16y₀ and 7y₀', '8y₀ and 7y₀', '4y₀ and 7y₀'], a: 1, e: '16y₀ − 9y₀ = 7y₀.' },
    { id: 'q84', p: ['t22'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Table 2.2, y₀ = (−½)gτ² is the position after the first interval τ.', 'Distances traversed in successive intervals are y₀, 3y₀, 5y₀, 7y₀, …'], a: 0, e: 'Both as per Table 2.2.' },
    { id: 'q85', p: ['sd1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A car moving at 36 km h⁻¹ stops in 20 m after brakes are applied. With the same deceleration, its stopping distance at 72 km h⁻¹ will be:', o: ['40 m', '160 m', '80 m', '20√2 m'], a: 2, e: 'dₛ ∝ v₀².', sol: ['dₛ = −v₀²/2a ⇒ dₛ ∝ v₀² (same a)', 'v₀ doubles: 36 → 72 km h⁻¹', 'dₛ becomes 2² = 4 times', 'Answer: 4 × 20 = 80 m'] },
    { id: 'q86', p: ['sd1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For a vehicle with initial velocity v₀ and deceleration −a, the stopping distance dₛ is:', o: ['v₀/2a', '−v₀/a', '−v₀²/2a', 'v₀²a/2'], a: 2, e: 'From v² = v₀² + 2ax with v = 0.' },
    { id: 'q87', p: ['sd1', 'rt1', 'ke4'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A driver moving at 20 m s⁻¹ sees an obstacle. His reaction time is 0.5 s and the brakes produce a deceleration of 5 m s⁻². The total distance travelled from seeing the obstacle till the car stops is:', o: ['40 m', '10 m', '60 m', '50 m'], a: 3, e: 'Distance during reaction time + braking distance.', sol: ['During reaction time (uniform motion): 20 × 0.5 = 10 m', 'Braking distance: dₛ = v₀²/2a = 400/(2 × 5) = 40 m', 'Total = 10 + 40', 'Answer: 50 m'] },
    { id: 'q88', p: ['sd1', 'tq2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A scooter moving at 54 km h⁻¹ is braked with a uniform deceleration of 3 m s⁻². The time taken to stop and the stopping distance are:', o: ['5 s and 37.5 m', '18 s and 486 m', '5 s and 75 m', '2.5 s and 37.5 m'], a: 0, e: 'Convert km h⁻¹ to m s⁻¹ first.', sol: ['54 km h⁻¹ = 54 × 5/18 = 15 m s⁻¹', 't = v₀/a = 15/3 = 5 s', 'dₛ = v₀²/2a = 225/6', 'Answer: 5 s and 37.5 m'] },
    { id: 'q89', p: ['sd2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'For a car of a particular make, braking distances 10, 20, 34 and 50 m for velocities 11, 15, 20 and 25 m s⁻¹ are:', o: ['Proportional to the velocity', 'Nearly consistent with dₛ ∝ v₀²', 'Independent of velocity', 'Inversely proportional to velocity'], a: 1, e: 'Example 2.6.' },
    { id: 'q90', p: ['sd2', 'sd1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Stopping distance is not considered while setting speed limits in school zones.', R: 'Stopping distance increases as the square of the initial velocity.' }, a: 3, e: 'Stopping distance is an important factor in setting speed limits.' },
    { id: 'q91', p: ['rt1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Reaction time is:', o: ['Time taken by the brakes to stop the car', 'Time for the car to stop completely', 'Time a person takes to observe, think and act', 'Time of fall of a ruler, always 1 s'], a: 2, e: 'Example 2.7.' },
    { id: 'q92', p: ['rt1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Reaction time is the time taken by the brakes to stop a car.', 'Reaction time is the same for every individual.'], a: 1, e: 'It is the time to observe, think and act, and depends on the individual.' },
    { id: 'q93', p: ['rt2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'In the ruler experiment (Fig 2.8), the ruler falls 21.0 cm before it is caught (g = 9.8 m s⁻²). The reaction time is about:', o: ['0.02 s', '2 s', '0.4 s', '0.2 s'], a: 3, e: 'tᵣ = √(2d/g).', sol: ['d = 21.0 cm = 0.21 m', 'tᵣ = √(2d/g) = √(2 × 0.21/9.8)', 'tᵣ = √0.0429 ≈ 0.207 s', 'Answer: ≅ 0.2 s'] },
    { id: 'q94', p: ['rt2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'If a person\'s reaction time is 0.3 s, the distance a dropped ruler falls before he catches it is (g = 9.8 m s⁻²):', o: ['29.4 cm', '88.2 cm', '4.41 cm', '44.1 cm'], a: 3, e: 'd = ½gtᵣ².', sol: ['Formula: d = ½ g tᵣ²', 'Substitution: d = ½ × 9.8 × (0.3)² = 4.9 × 0.09', 'd = 0.441 m', 'Answer: 44.1 cm'] },

    { id: 'q95', p: ['su1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For motion in a straight line, a position to the right of the origin is taken as:', o: ['Positive', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Undefined'], a: 0, e: 'Summary 1.' },
    { id: 'q96', p: ['su1', 'tq1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Average speed of an object is greater than or equal to the magnitude of its average velocity over a given interval.', R: 'Path length is greater than or equal to the magnitude of displacement.' }, a: 0, e: 'Both are divided by the same time interval.' },
    { id: 'q97', p: ['su2'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the motion with its graph.', match: { h: ['Motion / graph', 'Shape'], l: ['Uniform motion: v–t graph', 'Uniform acceleration: x–t graph', 'Uniform acceleration: v–t graph'], r: ['Straight line inclined to time axis', 'Straight line parallel to time axis', 'Parabola'] }, o: ['A-III, B-II, C-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II', 'A-II, B-I, C-III', 'A-II, B-III, C-I'], a: 3, e: 'Summary 4.' },
    { id: 'q98', p: ['su2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['For uniform motion, acceleration is zero and the x–t graph is a straight line inclined to the time axis.', 'For uniform acceleration, the v–t graph is a parabola.'], a: 2, e: 'v–t is a straight line inclined to time axis; x–t is a parabola.' },
    { id: 'q99', p: ['tq1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Dimension of displacement is:', o: ['[L]', '[LT⁻¹]', '[LT⁻²]', '[ML]'], a: 0, e: 'Same as path length.' },
    { id: 'q100', p: ['tq1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Path length and displacement both have dimension [L] and unit m.', 'In one dimension, the sign of displacement indicates its direction.'], a: 0, e: 'Table of quantities.' },
    { id: 'q101', p: ['tq2'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the quantity with its expression/dimension.', match: { h: ['Quantity', 'Expression / dimension'], l: ['Velocity', 'Acceleration', 'Average speed', 'Instantaneous acceleration'], r: ['dv/dt', 'Path length / time interval', '[LT⁻²]', '[LT⁻¹]'] }, o: ['A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I', 'A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II', 'A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV'], a: 2, e: 'Table of quantities.' },
    { id: 'q102', p: ['tq2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'An acceleration of 12960 km h⁻² expressed in m s⁻² is:', o: ['3.6 m s⁻²', '1 m s⁻²', '12.96 m s⁻²', '0.1 m s⁻²'], a: 1, e: 'Unit conversion: 1 h = 3600 s.', sol: ['12960 km h⁻² = 12960 × 1000 m / (3600 s)²', '= 1.296 × 10⁷ m / 1.296 × 10⁷ s²', '= 1 m s⁻²', 'Answer: 1 m s⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q103', p: ['tq2'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'A speed of 72 km h⁻¹ in SI units is:', o: ['20 m s⁻¹', '72 m s⁻¹', '36 m s⁻¹', '259.2 m s⁻¹'], a: 0, e: '1 km h⁻¹ = 5/18 m s⁻¹.', sol: ['1 km h⁻¹ = 1000 m / 3600 s = 5/18 m s⁻¹', '72 km h⁻¹ = 72 × 5/18', '= 4 × 5', 'Answer: 20 m s⁻¹'] },

    { id: 'q104', p: ['pp1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['The origin and the positive direction of an axis are a matter of choice.', 'This choice should be specified before assigning signs to displacement, velocity and acceleration.'], a: 0, e: 'Points to ponder 1.' },
    { id: 'q105', p: ['pp1', 'pp3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The sign of acceleration of a body in one-dimensional motion depends on:', o: ['Its mass', 'Magnitude of acceleration', 'Choice of positive direction of the axis', 'Time of motion'], a: 2, e: 'Points to ponder 3.' },
    { id: 'q106', p: ['pp2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'If the speed of a particle is decreasing, its acceleration is:', o: ['In the direction of velocity', 'Opposite to the direction of velocity', 'Always zero', 'Always positive'], a: 1, e: 'Points to ponder 2.' },
    { id: 'q107', p: ['pp2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A particle that is speeding up has acceleration in the direction of its velocity.', R: 'This statement depends on the choice of the origin and the axis.' }, a: 2, e: 'It is independent of the choice of origin and axis.' },
    { id: 'q108', p: ['pp3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The sign of acceleration tells whether the speed of a particle is increasing or decreasing.', R: 'The sign of acceleration depends on the choice of the positive direction of the axis.' }, a: 3, e: 'Points to ponder 3.' },
    { id: 'q109', p: ['pp3'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Taking vertically upward as the positive direction, which statements are correct?', s: ['Acceleration due to gravity is negative.', 'For a particle falling under gravity, this negative acceleration results in increase in speed.', 'For a particle thrown upward, the same negative acceleration results in decrease in speed.', 'The acceleration of a falling particle becomes positive.'], o: ['I, II and III only', 'I and IV only', 'II and III only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 0, e: 'g stays negative throughout.' },
    { id: 'q110', p: ['pp4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A particle thrown up has zero velocity at its uppermost point.', R: 'The acceleration of the particle at that point is zero.' }, a: 2, e: 'Acceleration continues to be g.' },
    { id: 'q111', p: ['pp4', 'xe4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'At the highest point of a ball thrown vertically upward:', o: ['v = 0, a = 0', 'v = 0, a = 9.8 m s⁻² downward', 'v = 9.8 m s⁻¹ upward, a = 0', 'v is maximum, a = 0'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 2.6(b).' },
    { id: 'q112', p: ['pp5'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In the kinematic equations of motion, displacement, velocity and acceleration are always taken as positive.', 'The kinematic equations apply to one-dimensional motion with constant acceleration when values are substituted with proper signs.'], a: 3, e: 'Quantities are algebraic.' },
    { id: 'q113', p: ['pp5', 'xe4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'Exercise 2.6(c): taking x = 0 at the highest point and vertically downward as positive, the signs of position, velocity and acceleration of the ball during its upward motion are:', o: ['+, −, +', '−, +, −', '+, +, +', '−, −, +'], a: 0, e: 'Below the top (x > 0), moving up (v < 0), g downward (a > 0).' },
    { id: 'q114', p: ['pp6'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Kinematic equations (2.9) can be applied directly even when the direction of acceleration changes during motion.', R: 'Kinematic equations are true only when magnitude and direction of acceleration are constant.' }, a: 3, e: 'Points to ponder 6.' },
    { id: 'q115', p: ['pp6'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The kinematic equations (2.9) are exact and correct for every type of motion.', 'The definition v = dx/dt is true only for constant acceleration.'], a: 1, e: 'Definitions are always exact; Eq. 2.9 needs constant acceleration.' },

    { id: 'q116', p: ['xe2'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 270 180" width="270" height="180" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="34.0" y1="155.0" x2="260.0" y2="155.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="260,155 253,151.5 253,158.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="34.0" y1="155.0" x2="34.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="34,12 30.5,19 37.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="258.0" y="148.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t</text><text x="40.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x</text><text x="28.0" y="169.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><line x1="31.0" y1="74.0" x2="37.0" y2="74.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="28.0" y="78.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">P</text><line x1="31.0" y1="33.5" x2="37.0" y2="33.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="28.0" y="37.5" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Q</text><polyline points="34.0,155.0 244.0,74.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="97.0,155.0 202.0,33.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="166.5" y="94.3" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">A</text><text x="162.6" y="67.2" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">B</text><line x1="244.0" y1="74.0" x2="244.0" y2="155.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="34.0" y1="74.0" x2="244.0" y2="74.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="202.0" y1="33.5" x2="202.0" y2="155.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="34.0" y1="33.5" x2="202.0" y2="33.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'The x–t graphs of two children A and B returning from school O to their homes P and Q are shown. Which statements are correct?', s: ['A lives closer to the school than B.', 'A starts from the school earlier than B.', 'B walks faster than A.', 'A and B reach home at the same time.'], o: ['I and II only', 'I, II and III only', 'II, III and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 2.2.' },
    { id: 'q117', p: ['xe2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 270 180" width="270" height="180" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="34.0" y1="155.0" x2="260.0" y2="155.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="260,155 253,151.5 253,158.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="34.0" y1="155.0" x2="34.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="34,12 30.5,19 37.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="258.0" y="148.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t</text><text x="40.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x</text><text x="28.0" y="169.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><line x1="31.0" y1="74.0" x2="37.0" y2="74.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="28.0" y="78.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">P</text><line x1="31.0" y1="33.5" x2="37.0" y2="33.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="28.0" y="37.5" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Q</text><polyline points="34.0,155.0 244.0,74.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="97.0,155.0 202.0,33.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><text x="166.5" y="94.3" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">A</text><text x="162.6" y="67.2" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">B</text><line x1="244.0" y1="74.0" x2="244.0" y2="155.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="34.0" y1="74.0" x2="244.0" y2="74.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="202.0" y1="33.5" x2="202.0" y2="155.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="34.0" y1="33.5" x2="202.0" y2="33.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'In the same graph, on the road:', o: ['A overtakes B once', 'B overtakes A once', 'B overtakes A twice', 'They never meet'], a: 1, e: 'Lines cross once; B is faster.' },
    { id: 'q118', p: ['xe3', 'ke4'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'A car moving at 90 km h⁻¹ is brought to rest in 125 m with uniform retardation. The retardation is:', o: ['2.5 m s⁻²', '5 m s⁻²', '1.25 m s⁻²', '0.72 m s⁻²'], a: 0, e: 'Convert units first.', sol: ['90 km h⁻¹ = 90 × 5/18 = 25 m s⁻¹', 'v² = v₀² − 2ax ⇒ 0 = 625 − 2a(125)', 'a = 625/250', 'Answer: 2.5 m s⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q119', p: ['xe4'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A player throws a ball upwards with 29.4 m s⁻¹ (g = 9.8 m s⁻²). The height to which it rises and the time after which it returns to his hands are:', o: ['44.1 m and 3 s', '88.2 m and 6 s', '44.1 m and 6 s', '29.4 m and 3 s'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 2.6(d).', sol: ['Height: h = v₀²/2g = (29.4)²/19.6 = 864.36/19.6 = 44.1 m', 'Time to top: t = v₀/g = 29.4/9.8 = 3 s', 'Time to come back = same 3 s', 'Answer: 44.1 m and 6 s'] },
    { id: 'q120', p: ['xe4', 'pp2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'During the upward motion of a ball thrown vertically up, the direction of acceleration is:', o: ['Vertically upward', 'Zero', 'Vertically downward', 'Horizontal'], a: 2, e: 'Acceleration due to gravity is always downward.' },
    { id: 'q121', p: ['xe5'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 3, q: 'For a particle in one-dimensional motion, which statements are true?', s: ['With zero speed at an instant it may have non-zero acceleration at that instant.', 'With zero speed it may have non-zero velocity.', 'With constant speed it must have zero acceleration.', 'With positive value of acceleration it must be speeding up.'], o: ['II and IV only', 'I only', 'I and III only', 'I, III and IV only'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 2.7.' },
    { id: 'q122', p: ['xe5', 'pp3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'A particle with positive value of acceleration must be speeding up.', R: 'If velocity is negative and acceleration positive, the particle slows down.' }, a: 3, e: 'R gives the counter-example.' },
    { id: 'q123', p: ['xe6'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A man walks 2.5 km to a market at 5 km h⁻¹, finds it closed and walks back at 7.5 km h⁻¹. For 0–50 min, the magnitude of average velocity and average speed are:', o: ['6 km h⁻¹ and 6 km h⁻¹', '0 and 6 km h⁻¹', '0 and 6.25 km h⁻¹', '3 km h⁻¹ and 6 km h⁻¹'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 2.10.', sol: ['Time to market = 2.5/5 = 0.5 h = 30 min; back = 2.5/7.5 h = 20 min', 'At 50 min he is home: displacement = 0 ⇒ average velocity = 0', 'Path length = 5 km in 50 min = 5/6 h', 'Average speed = 5 ÷ (5/6) = 6 km h⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q124', p: ['xe6'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'For the same man, over 0–40 min, the magnitude of average velocity and the average speed are:', o: ['5.625 km h⁻¹ and 1.875 km h⁻¹', '3.75 km h⁻¹ and 5.625 km h⁻¹', '1.875 km h⁻¹ and 6.25 km h⁻¹', '1.875 km h⁻¹ and 5.625 km h⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 2.10(iii).', sol: ['30–40 min (1/6 h) walking back at 7.5 km h⁻¹ covers 1.25 km', 'Displacement = 2.5 − 1.25 = 1.25 km; path = 2.5 + 1.25 = 3.75 km', 'Time = 40 min = 2/3 h', '|v̄| = 1.25 ÷ (2/3) = 1.875 km h⁻¹; speed = 3.75 ÷ (2/3) = 5.625 km h⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q125', p: ['xe7'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 250 170" width="250" height="170" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="34.0" y1="90.0" x2="240.0" y2="90.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="240,90 233,86.5 233,93.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="34.0" y1="160.0" x2="34.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="34,12 30.5,19 37.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="238.0" y="83.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t</text><text x="40.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Speed</text><polyline points="34.0,90.0 35.6,86.2 37.2,82.4 38.8,78.6 40.4,74.9 42.0,71.3 43.6,67.8 45.2,64.5 46.8,61.3 48.4,58.3 50.0,55.6 51.6,53.0 53.2,50.7 54.8,48.6 56.4,46.8 58.0,45.3 59.6,44.0 61.2,43.1 62.8,42.4 64.4,42.1 66.0,42.0 67.6,42.3 69.2,42.9 70.8,43.7 72.4,44.9 74.0,46.4 75.6,48.1 77.2,50.1 78.8,52.4 80.4,54.9 82.0,57.6 83.6,60.5 85.2,63.6 86.8,66.9 88.4,70.4 90.0,73.9 91.6,77.6 93.2,81.3 94.8,85.1 96.4,89.0 98.0,92.8 99.6,96.6 101.2,100.4 102.8,104.1 104.4,107.7 106.0,111.2 107.6,114.6 109.2,117.8 110.8,120.9 112.4,123.7 114.0,126.3 115.6,128.7 117.2,130.9 118.8,132.7 120.4,134.4 122.0,135.7 123.6,136.7 125.2,137.4 126.8,137.9 128.4,138.0 130.0,137.8 131.6,137.3 133.2,136.5 134.8,135.4 136.4,134.1 138.0,132.4 139.6,130.5 141.2,128.3 142.8,125.8 144.4,123.2 146.0,120.3 147.6,117.2 149.2,114.0 150.8,110.6 152.4,107.1 154.0,103.4 155.6,99.7 157.2,95.9 158.8,92.1 160.4,88.2 162.0,84.4 163.6,80.6 165.2,76.9 166.8,73.2 168.4,69.7 170.0,66.3 171.6,63.0 173.2,59.9 174.8,57.0 176.4,54.4 178.0,51.9 179.6,49.7 181.2,47.7 182.8,46.1 184.4,44.7 186.0,43.5 187.6,42.7 189.2,42.2 190.8,42.0 192.4,42.1 194.0,42.5 195.6,43.2 197.2,44.2 198.8,45.5 200.4,47.1 202.0,49.0 203.6,51.1 205.2,53.5 206.8,56.1 208.4,58.9 210.0,61.9 211.6,65.1 213.2,68.5 214.8,72.0 216.4,75.6 218.0,79.3 219.6,83.1 221.2,86.9 222.8,90.7 224.4,94.6 226.0,98.4" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>', q: 'The speed–time graph shown cannot represent one-dimensional motion of a particle because:', o: ['Speed cannot be negative', 'Speed cannot change with time', 'The graph must be a straight line', 'Time axis is drawn wrongly'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 2.12 / Fig 2.10(c).' },
    { id: 'q126', p: ['xe7'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 3, q: 'Which of the following graphs cannot possibly represent one-dimensional motion of a particle?', s: ['An x–t graph showing two different positions at the same instant.', 'A v–t graph showing two different velocities at the same instant.', 'A speed–time graph having negative values of speed.', 'A total path length–time graph that decreases with time.'], o: ['I and II only', 'I, II and III only', 'III and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 3, e: 'Fig 2.10 (a)–(d): none is possible.' },
    { id: 'q127', p: ['xe8'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A police van moving at 30 km h⁻¹ fires a bullet at a thief\'s car speeding away in the same direction at 192 km h⁻¹. If the muzzle speed of the bullet is 150 m s⁻¹, the speed with which the bullet hits the car is:', o: ['150 m s⁻¹', '105 m s⁻¹', '195 m s⁻¹', '203 m s⁻¹'], a: 1, e: 'Use speed of bullet relative to the car.', sol: ['Van: 30 km h⁻¹ = 8.33 m s⁻¹; car: 192 km h⁻¹ = 53.33 m s⁻¹', 'Bullet speed w.r.t. ground = 150 + 8.33 = 158.33 m s⁻¹', 'Relative to car = 158.33 − 53.33', 'Answer: 105 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q128', p: ['xe8', 'in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In Exercise 2.14, the speed relevant for damaging the thief\'s car is the speed of the bullet:', o: ['Relative to the ground', 'Relative to the police van', 'Equal to the muzzle speed', 'Relative to the thief\'s car'], a: 3, e: 'Relative velocity concept.' },
    { id: 'q129', p: ['xe9'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A drunkard takes 5 steps forward and 3 steps backward, again and again. Each step is 1 m long and takes 1 s. He falls into a pit 13 m away from the start after:', o: ['13 s', '37 s', '29 s', '40 s'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 2.4.', sol: ['One cycle (8 steps) = 8 s, net displacement = 5 − 3 = 2 m', 'After 4 cycles (32 s) he is at 8 m', 'Needs 5 m more = 5 forward steps = 5 s', 'Answer: 32 + 5 = 37 s'] },
    { id: 'q130', p: ['xe9'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'For the same drunkard (5 steps forward, 3 back, 1 m and 1 s per step), how long does he take to fall into a pit 7 m away?', o: ['13 s', '21 s', '8 s', '15 s'], a: 0, e: 'Check the forward part of the cycle.', sol: ['After first cycle (8 s) he is at 2 m', 'Next 5 forward steps: 2 → 7 m', 'Time = 8 + 5', 'Answer: 13 s (not 7/2 × 8 — he reaches the pit while moving forward)'] },
    { id: 'q131', p: ['xe10'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'A woman stays at her office (2.5 km from home) up to 5.00 pm and returns by an auto with speed 25 km h⁻¹. She reaches home at:', o: ['5.10 pm', '5.30 pm', '5.06 pm', '5.01 pm'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 2.3.', sol: ['Time = distance / speed = 2.5/25 h', '= 0.1 h', '= 6 min', 'Answer: 5.06 pm'] },
    { id: 'q132', p: ['xe10'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In the x–t graph of the woman (Exercise 2.3), the part between 9.30 am and 5.00 pm when she stays in the office is:', o: ['A straight line parallel to the time axis', 'A parabola', 'A line inclined to the time axis', 'A vertical line'], a: 0, e: 'Position does not change.' },
    { id: 'q133', p: ['xe11'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 250 160" width="250" height="160" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="125.0" y1="130.0" x2="240.0" y2="130.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="240,130 233,126.5 233,133.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="125.0" y1="130.0" x2="125.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="125,12 121.5,19 128.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="238.0" y="123.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t</text><text x="131.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x</text><line x1="14.0" y1="130.0" x2="125.0" y2="130.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="118.0" y="145.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">0</text><polyline points="19.4,129.5 125.0,129.5" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><polyline points="125.0,130.0 126.6,130.0 128.2,129.9 129.8,129.7 131.4,129.5 133.0,129.2 134.6,128.9 136.2,128.4 137.8,128.0 139.4,127.4 141.0,126.8 142.6,126.1 144.2,125.4 145.8,124.6 147.4,123.8 149.0,122.8 150.6,121.8 152.2,120.8 153.8,119.7 155.4,118.5 157.0,117.3 158.6,116.0 160.2,114.6 161.8,113.2 163.4,111.7 165.0,110.1 166.6,108.5 168.2,106.8 169.8,105.0 171.4,103.2 173.0,101.3 174.6,99.4 176.2,97.4 177.8,95.3 179.4,93.2 181.0,91.0 182.6,88.7 184.2,86.4 185.8,84.0 187.4,81.6 189.0,79.0 190.6,76.5 192.2,73.8 193.8,71.1 195.4,68.3 197.0,65.5 198.6,62.6 200.2,59.6 201.8,56.6 203.4,53.5 205.0,50.4 206.6,47.2 208.2,43.9 209.8,40.5 211.4,37.1 213.0,33.7 214.6,30.1 216.2,26.5 217.8,22.9 219.4,19.1 221.0,15.3" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>', q: 'The x–t plot shown is like Fig 2.11. Which statement is correct?', o: ['The particle moves in a straight line for t < 0 and on a parabolic path for t > 0', 'The particle moves with constant velocity for t > 0', 'The particle is at rest for t < 0 and moves in a straight line with uniform acceleration for t > 0', 'The particle moves in a circle'], a: 2, e: 'x–t graph does not show the path.' },
    { id: 'q134', p: ['xe11'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Fig 2.11, the particle is at rest for t < 0.', 'The shape of an x–t graph is the shape of the path of the particle.'], a: 2, e: 'e.g. a car starting from rest has a parabolic x–t graph but a straight path.' },

    { id: 'q135', p: ['iv1', 'sp1', 'pp4'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'x = t³ − 6t² + 9t (SI units). The distance travelled by the particle from t = 0 to t = 4 s is:', o: ['12 m', '4 m', '8 m', '16 m'], a: 0, e: 'Find where the particle turns back.', sol: ['v = dx/dt = 3t² − 12t + 9 = 3(t − 1)(t − 3) ⇒ turns at t = 1 s and 3 s', 'x(0) = 0, x(1) = 4 m, x(3) = 0, x(4) = 4 m', 'Distance = |4 − 0| + |0 − 4| + |4 − 0| = 4 + 4 + 4', 'Answer: 12 m (displacement is only 4 m)'] },
    { id: 'q136', p: ['ac3', 'pp4'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For x = t³ − 6t² + 9t (SI units), the acceleration at the first instant when velocity is zero is:', o: ['6 m s⁻²', '−6 m s⁻²', 'Zero', '−12 m s⁻²'], a: 1, e: 'Zero velocity, non-zero acceleration.', sol: ['v = 3t² − 12t + 9 = 0 ⇒ t = 1 s (first)', 'a = dv/dt = 6t − 12', 'At t = 1 s: a = 6 − 12', 'Answer: −6 m s⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q137', p: ['ke2', 'ke3', 'ac6'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A particle starts from rest, accelerates at 4 m s⁻² for 5 s and then decelerates uniformly to rest in the next 10 s. Total distance travelled is:', o: ['100 m', '200 m', '150 m', '250 m'], a: 2, e: 'Area of the triangle under v–t graph.', sol: ['Maximum velocity: v = 0 + 4 × 5 = 20 m s⁻¹', 'First part: x₁ = ½ × 4 × 25 = 50 m', 'Second part: x₂ = [(20 + 0)/2] × 10 = 100 m', 'Total = 150 m (= ½ × 15 × 20, area under v–t)'] },
    { id: 'q138', p: ['ff2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Two balls are dropped from the same height, the second one 1 s after the first (g = 10 m s⁻²). Their separation when the first ball has fallen for 3 s is:', o: ['5 m', '25 m', '45 m', '10 m'], a: 1, e: 'Use y = ½gt² for each.', sol: ['First ball (3 s): ½ × 10 × 9 = 45 m', 'Second ball (2 s): ½ × 10 × 4 = 20 m', 'Separation = 45 − 20', 'Answer: 25 m'] },
    { id: 'q139', p: ['ex23a', 'pp4'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A ball thrown vertically upward returns to the thrower after 4 s (g = 10 m s⁻²). The maximum height reached is:', o: ['40 m', '80 m', '10 m', '20 m'], a: 3, e: 'Time up = time down.', sol: ['Time to top = 4/2 = 2 s', 'v₀ = gt = 10 × 2 = 20 m s⁻¹', 'H = v₀²/2g = 400/20', 'Answer: 20 m'] },
    { id: 'q140', p: ['sd1', 'ke4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'If the initial speed of a vehicle is doubled, its stopping distance becomes four times (same deceleration).', R: 'Stopping distance dₛ = −v₀/2a.' }, a: 2, e: 'dₛ = −v₀²/2a.' },
    { id: 'q141', p: ['ac2', 'ac4'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A body moving at +15 m s⁻¹ along x-axis is found moving at −5 m s⁻¹ after 4 s. Its average acceleration is:', o: ['−2.5 m s⁻²', '−5 m s⁻²', '+5 m s⁻²', '−20 m s⁻²'], a: 1, e: 'Signs matter: velocity changed direction.', sol: ['ā = (v₂ − v₁)/(t₂ − t₁)', '= (−5 − 15)/4', '= −20/4', 'Answer: −5 m s⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q142', p: ['ac5', 'ac3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 240 150" width="240" height="150" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38.0" y1="120.0" x2="230.0" y2="120.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="230,120 223,116.5 223,123.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="38.0" y1="120.0" x2="38.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="38,12 34.5,19 41.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="228.0" y="113.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t (s)</text><text x="44.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">a (m s⁻²)</text><line x1="188.0" y1="117.0" x2="188.0" y2="123.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="188.0" y="134.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">5</text><line x1="35.0" y1="60.0" x2="41.0" y2="60.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="64.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">2</text><polyline points="38.0,60.0 188.0,60.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="188.0" y1="60.0" x2="188.0" y2="120.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="60.0" x2="188.0" y2="60.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'The a–t graph of a body starting from rest is shown. Its velocity at t = 5 s is:', o: ['2 m s⁻¹', '25 m s⁻¹', '10 m s⁻¹', '5 m s⁻¹'], a: 2, e: 'Constant acceleration: v = v₀ + at.', sol: ['From graph: a = 2 m s⁻² (constant), v₀ = 0', 'v = v₀ + at', 'v = 0 + 2 × 5', 'Answer: 10 m s⁻¹ (equal to area under a–t graph)'] },
    { id: 'q143', p: ['sp1', 'su1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 250 160" width="250" height="160" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38.0" y1="135.0" x2="240.0" y2="135.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="240,135 233,131.5 233,138.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="38.0" y1="135.0" x2="38.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="38,12 34.5,19 41.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="238.0" y="128.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t (s)</text><text x="44.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x (m)</text><line x1="98.0" y1="132.0" x2="98.0" y2="138.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="98.0" y="149.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">2</text><line x1="158.0" y1="132.0" x2="158.0" y2="138.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="158.0" y="149.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">4</text><line x1="218.0" y1="132.0" x2="218.0" y2="138.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="218.0" y="149.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">6</text><line x1="35.0" y1="35.0" x2="41.0" y2="35.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="39.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">10</text><polyline points="38.0,135.0 98.0,35.0 158.0,35.0 218.0,135.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="98.0" y1="35.0" x2="98.0" y2="135.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="35.0" x2="98.0" y2="35.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="158.0" y1="35.0" x2="158.0" y2="135.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="35.0" x2="158.0" y2="35.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'The x–t graph of a particle is shown. Its average speed and average velocity over 0–6 s are:', o: ['0 and 3.33 m s⁻¹', '5 m s⁻¹ and 0', '3.33 m s⁻¹ and 0', '3.33 m s⁻¹ and 1.67 m s⁻¹'], a: 2, e: 'Particle returns to the origin.', sol: ['Path length = 10 m (going) + 0 (rest) + 10 m (returning) = 20 m', 'Displacement = x(6) − x(0) = 0', 'Average speed = 20/6 ≈ 3.33 m s⁻¹', 'Average velocity = 0/6 = 0'] },
    { id: 'q144', p: ['iv1', 'pp1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 250 160" width="250" height="160" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38.0" y1="135.0" x2="240.0" y2="135.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="240,135 233,131.5 233,138.5" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="38.0" y1="135.0" x2="38.0" y2="12.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="38,12 34.5,19 41.5,19" fill="currentColor"/><text x="238.0" y="128.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">t (s)</text><text x="44.0" y="18.0" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">x (m)</text><line x1="98.0" y1="132.0" x2="98.0" y2="138.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="98.0" y="149.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">2</text><line x1="158.0" y1="132.0" x2="158.0" y2="138.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="158.0" y="149.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">4</text><line x1="218.0" y1="132.0" x2="218.0" y2="138.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="218.0" y="149.0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">6</text><line x1="35.0" y1="35.0" x2="41.0" y2="35.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="32.0" y="39.0" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">10</text><polyline points="38.0,135.0 98.0,35.0 158.0,35.0 218.0,135.0" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="98.0" y1="35.0" x2="98.0" y2="135.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="35.0" x2="98.0" y2="35.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="158.0" y1="35.0" x2="158.0" y2="135.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="38.0" y1="35.0" x2="158.0" y2="35.0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/></svg>', q: 'In the same x–t graph, the velocity of the particle between t = 4 s and t = 6 s is:', o: ['+5 m s⁻¹', '−5 m s⁻¹', 'Zero', '−10 m s⁻¹'], a: 1, e: 'Slope of x–t graph.', sol: ['Velocity = slope of x–t graph', '= (0 − 10)/(6 − 4)', '= −10/2', 'Answer: −5 m s⁻¹ (moving towards origin)'] },
    { id: 'q145', p: ['ac4', 'pp2', 'pp4'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 3, q: 'For one-dimensional motion, which statements are correct?', s: ['A body can have zero velocity and non-zero acceleration at an instant.', 'A body moving with constant non-zero speed can have non-zero acceleration.', 'A body can have velocity and acceleration in opposite directions.', 'A body with negative acceleration always slows down.'], o: ['I, II and III only', 'II and IV only', 'I and IV only', 'I and III only'], a: 3, e: 'II false in 1D; IV depends on sign of velocity.' },
    { id: 'q146', p: ['ke2', 'ke5'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A particle moving with uniform acceleration covers 24 m in the first 4 s and 64 m in the next 4 s. Its initial velocity and acceleration are:', o: ['2.5 m s⁻¹ and 1 m s⁻²', '1 m s⁻¹ and 5 m s⁻²', '2 m s⁻¹ and 2.5 m s⁻²', '1 m s⁻¹ and 2.5 m s⁻²'], a: 3, e: 'Two equations, two unknowns.', sol: ['x = v₀t + ½at²: for 4 s, 24 = 4v₀ + 8a', 'For 8 s, 24 + 64 = 88 = 8v₀ + 32a', 'Double first: 48 = 8v₀ + 16a; subtract: 40 = 16a ⇒ a = 2.5 m s⁻²', 'Then 24 = 4v₀ + 20 ⇒ v₀ = 1 m s⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q147', p: ['f21', 'ac3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For the car of Fig 2.1 (x = 0.08 t³), the instantaneous acceleration at t = 4 s is:', o: ['1.92 m s⁻²', '3.84 m s⁻²', '0.96 m s⁻²', '0.48 m s⁻²'], a: 0, e: 'Differentiate twice.', sol: ['v = dx/dt = 0.24 t²', 'a = dv/dt = 0.48 t', 'a(4) = 0.48 × 4', 'Answer: 1.92 m s⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q148', p: ['in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'To understand the relative nature of motion, the chapter introduces the concept of:', o: ['Momentum', 'Relative velocity', 'Kinetic energy', 'Projectile motion'], a: 1, e: 'NCERT 2.1.' },
    { id: 'q149', p: ['ff1', 'pp3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A freely falling body has negative acceleration when upward is taken positive, yet its speed increases.', R: 'Speed increases when acceleration is in the direction of velocity.' }, a: 0, e: 'Both velocity and acceleration are downward.' },
    { id: 'q150', p: ['gl1', 't22'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A body falls freely from rest. The ratio of distances fallen in the first 2 s and the next 2 s is:', o: ['1 : 2', '1 : 3', '1 : 4', '1 : 5'], a: 1, e: 'Odd-number rule with τ = 2 s.', sol: ['Distance in time t from rest ∝ t²', 'First 2 s: ∝ 2² = 4; first 4 s: ∝ 4² = 16', 'Next 2 s: ∝ 16 − 4 = 12', 'Ratio = 4 : 12 = 1 : 3'] },
    { id: 'q151', p: ['ex23b', 'ke4'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'In Example 2.3 (20 m s⁻¹ upward from a 25 m high building, g = 10 m s⁻²), the speed of the ball just before it hits the ground is:', o: ['20 m s⁻¹', '25 m s⁻¹', '45 m s⁻¹', '30 m s⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'v² = v₀² + 2a(y − y₀).', sol: ['v₀ = +20 m s⁻¹, a = −10 m s⁻², y − y₀ = 0 − 25 = −25 m', 'v² = 400 + 2(−10)(−25) = 400 + 500', 'v² = 900', 'Answer: 30 m s⁻¹'] }
  ]
});
